window.DAR = window.DAR || {};

DAR.SUMMARIES = Object.assign(DAR.SUMMARIES || {}, {

  "ai310.0.0": {
    takeaway: "CS336 teaches language models by building them, because the frontier has become an industry whose details are unpublished and whose behaviour changes with scale. What transfers from small models is mechanics and mindset — above all efficiency, since accuracy is efficiency times resources. The first piece built is the tokenizer, and byte-pair encoding wins because it learns its vocabulary from the corpus.",
    beats: [
      { t: "Why build from scratch", d: "Eight years ago researchers trained their own models, six years ago they fine-tuned BERT, today they prompt a proprietary model. Those abstractions are leaky, and fundamental research still means tearing up the stack — so the course teaches understanding via building." },
      { t: "Industrialised, closed, and different at scale", d: "GPT-4 reportedly has 1.8T parameters and cost 100M dollars to train, and its technical report gives no details. Small models may not be representative: the share of FLOPs in attention versus the MLP changes with scale, and some behaviours emerge only at scale." },
      { t: "What transfers", d: "Mechanics (what a Transformer is, how parallelism uses GPUs) and mindset (squeezing the hardware, taking scale seriously) transfer; intuitions about data and modelling only partly. The bitter lesson, read right: algorithms that scale matter, because accuracy = efficiency × resources — so maximise efficiency." },
      { t: "How we got here", d: "Shannon's entropy of English and n-gram models; then neural LMs, seq2seq, Adam, attention, the Transformer, mixture of experts and model parallelism; ELMo, BERT and T5; GPT-2, scaling laws, GPT-3, PaLM, Chinchilla; then open models — The Pile, OPT, BLOOM, Llama, Qwen, DeepSeek, OLMo — at three levels of openness: closed, open-weight, open-source." },
      { t: "The course, in five parts", d: "Basics (tokenizer, Transformer variants, training); systems (kernels, parallelism, inference with its prefill and decode phases); scaling laws (D* = 20 N*, ignoring inference cost); data (evaluation, curation, filtering, deduplication); alignment (SFT, then learning from preferences or verifiers with PPO, DPO or GRPO). Five assignments, no scaffolding." },
      { t: "Efficiency drives the design", d: "Today we are compute-constrained: filter out bad data, tokenize rather than use raw bytes, choose architectures that save memory or FLOPs, train for a single epoch, tune hyperparameters on small models, and align smaller base models. Tomorrow we will be data-constrained." },
      { t: "Character, byte and word tokenizers", d: "A tokenizer maps strings to integer sequences and back. Characters: about 150K code points, many rare. Bytes: a tidy 256-symbol vocabulary but a compression ratio of 1, so sequences grow long while attention is quadratic. Words: an unbounded vocabulary, rare words learned poorly, and unseen ones mapped to UNK." },
      { t: "Byte-pair encoding", d: "Train the tokenizer on raw text: start with every byte as a token and repeatedly merge the most common adjacent pair, so common sequences become one token and rare ones many. GPT-2 runs BPE within regex pre-tokenized segments; the GPT-2 tokenizer packs 'Hello, 🌍! 你好!' at about 1.67 bytes per token." },
    ],
    worked: "Training BPE on 'the cat in the hat' with 3 merges: count adjacent byte pairs, merge the most common (t, h) into token 256; recount, merge (256, e) into 257; recount, merge (257, space) into 258. Encoding new text replays the merges in order — 'the quick brown fox' becomes 258 followed by the 15 remaining bytes.",
    watch: "Reading the bitter lesson as 'only scale matters'. At large scale waste is unaffordable, so efficient algorithms matter more, not less — the course's question is always the best model for a given compute and data budget.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Lecture 1 sorts what a small-scale course can teach into mechanics, mindset and intuitions. Which does it say transfer to frontier models?", opts: ["All three, since a Transformer works the very same way at every single scale", "Only intuitions, as the mechanics change with each new architecture", "None, since small models simply aren't representative of large ones", "Mechanics and mindset do; intuitions only partly, as they shift with scale"], a: 3, src: "lecture_01.py, why_this_course_exists",
        expl: "Mechanics (what a Transformer is, how model parallelism uses GPUs) and mindset (squeezing the most out of the hardware, taking scale seriously) transfer. Intuitions about which data and modelling decisions give good accuracy only partly do: more is different — the share of FLOPs in attention, and emergent behaviour, change with scale." },
      { q: "What does the lecture call the right reading of 'the bitter lesson'?", opts: ["Algorithms that scale are what matters: accuracy = efficiency × resources", "Scale is all that matters, so algorithmic improvements can be ignored", "Hand-built knowledge beats learned features once there is enough compute", "Smaller models win, because they are cheaper to run at inference time"], a: 0, src: "lecture_01.py, why_this_course_exists",
        expl: "The wrong reading is that only scale matters and algorithms don't. The right one: algorithms that scale. Since accuracy = efficiency × resources, efficiency matters even more at large scale, where waste is unaffordable — hence the framing, the best model for a given compute and data budget." },
      { q: "The lecture splits inference into prefill and decode. How do the two phases differ?", opts: ["Prefill trains the KV cache's weights; decode freezes them to sample", "Prefill handles the prompt all at once; decode emits one token at a time", "Prefill runs on a small draft model; decode always uses the full model", "Prefill happens only during training; decode is the one phase used in serving"], a: 1, src: "lecture_01.py, systems (Inference)",
        expl: "Prefill is like training: the prompt's tokens are all given, so they are processed together and the work is compute-bound. Decode must generate one token at a time, which is memory-bound. Speed-ups: cheaper models (pruning, quantization, distillation), speculative decoding — exact, using a draft model — and KV caching and batching." },
      { q: "By the lecture's TL;DR, the compute-optimal token count is D* = 20 N*. Training compute is proportional to N × D. If your FLOPs budget grows 4-fold, by what factor should the compute-optimal model size N* grow?", num: 2, src: "lecture_01.py, scaling_laws",
        expl: "With D* = 20 N*, compute is proportional to N* × 20 N* = 20 N*², so N* grows with the square root of the budget: √4 = 2, and the tokens double too — the extra compute is split between a bigger model and more data, as in the lecture's example of a 1.4B model on 28B tokens. The lecture adds a caveat: the rule ignores inference costs." },
      { q: "What is the character tokenizer's compression ratio — UTF-8 bytes per token — on 'Hello, 🌍! 你好!'? Two decimals.", num: 1.54, tol: 0.01, src: "lecture_01.py, character_tokenizer and byte_tokenizer",
        expl: "One token per character: H, e, l, l, o, comma, space, 🌍, !, space, 你, 好, ! — 13 tokens. In UTF-8 the 10 ASCII characters take a byte each, 🌍 takes four and each Chinese character three: 20 bytes, and 20 / 13 ≈ 1.54. The byte tokenizer scores exactly 1; the GPT-2 tokenizer, 20 / 12 ≈ 1.67." },
      { q: "train_bpe on 'the cat in the hat' with 3 merges learns t + h, then th + e, then 'the' + space. How many tokens does encoding 'the quick brown fox' (19 bytes) give?", num: 16, src: "lecture_01.py, bpe_tokenizer and train_bpe; var/traces/lecture_01.json",
        expl: "Only the leading 'the ' can merge: t + h → 256, 256 + e → 257, 257 + space → 258, so 4 bytes become 1 token. The other 15 bytes stay single tokens: 1 + 15 = 16, as the recorded trace shows ([258, 113, 117, ...])." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.1": {
    takeaway: "Training is resource accounting: every tensor costs bytes set by its count and data type, every matmul costs 2 × m × n × p FLOPs, and a training step costs about 6 × tokens × parameters. With those two ledgers — memory and compute — you can answer how long a run takes and how big a model fits before writing a line of model code.",
    beats: [
      { t: "Napkin math first", d: "Training a 70B model on 15T tokens is 6 × 70e9 × 15e12 = 6.3e24 FLOPs; 1024 H100s at half their 989.5 TFLOP/s dense peak deliver about 4.4e22 FLOPs a day, so roughly 144 days. AdamW in float32 needs 16 bytes per parameter, so 8 H100s hold about 40B parameters — before activations." },
      { t: "Floating-point types", d: "float32 is 4 bytes and the default. float16 halves that but underflows: 1e-8 becomes 0. bfloat16 keeps 2 bytes with float32's dynamic range and coarser resolution, which deep learning tolerates. FP8 (E4M3, E5M2) was standardised in 2022. Lower precision saves memory and compute but risks instability." },
      { t: "Tensors are views into storage", d: "A tensor is a pointer into memory plus strides: in a 4 × 4 matrix the next row is 4 elements on, the next column 1. Slicing, view and transpose make views — free, and sharing memory, so mutating one changes the other. A transposed tensor is non-contiguous, so it must be copied with contiguous() before another view." },
      { t: "einops: name the dimensions", d: "x @ y.transpose(-2, -1) hides what -2 and -1 mean. jaxtyping annotations document shapes; einsum names every axis and sums those missing from the output; reduce collapses one; rearrange splits or merges, such as (heads hidden) into heads and hidden." },
      { t: "Counting FLOPs", d: "FLOPs measure work, FLOP/s measure hardware speed. A matmul of B × D by D × K is 2BDK FLOPs, and nothing else in deep learning is as expensive at scale, so the forward pass is about 2 × tokens × parameters. MFU — actual over promised FLOP/s — of 0.5 or more is good; bfloat16 runs much faster than float32." },
      { t: "Backward is twice forward", d: "For each weight matrix the backward pass needs one matmul for the weight's gradient and one for its input's: 4 × points × parameters. Forward plus backward: 6 × points × parameters per step." },
      { t: "Parameters, initialisation, data", d: "Parameters are nn.Parameter tensors. Unit-variance weights make outputs grow like sqrt(input_dim), so scale by 1/sqrt(input_dim) (Xavier, up to a constant) and truncate outliers. Seed torch, NumPy and Python's random; memmap token arrays instead of loading terabytes; pin memory to copy batches to the GPU asynchronously." },
      { t: "Optimizers, checkpoints, mixed precision", d: "Momentum averages gradients, AdaGrad divides by accumulated squared gradients, RMSProp averages those exponentially, Adam is RMSProp plus momentum — and the state costs memory. Save model and optimizer state regularly, since long runs crash. Mixed precision: bfloat16 or fp8 for the forward pass, float32 for parameters and gradients." },
    ],
    worked: "Memory for training a model of P parameters with AdamW in float32: 4P for parameters + 4P for gradients + 8P for the two optimizer moments = 16P bytes, plus activations, which grow with batch size and sequence length. Compute per step: 6 × (tokens in the batch) × P FLOPs; divide by the hardware's FLOP/s times MFU for wall-clock time.",
    watch: "Quoting an H100's 1979 TFLOP/s: that figure assumes sparsity, and dense matmuls get half. And forgetting that FLOP/s depend on data type — the same GPU is many times slower in float32 than in bfloat16.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Why does the lecture prefer bfloat16 to float16 for deep learning?", opts: ["It uses half of float16's memory while keeping the same precision", "It has a finer resolution than float32, so small gradients stay exact", "Same memory as float16 with float32's range; only resolution suffers", "H100s multiply only in bfloat16, so float16 runs at a quarter speed"], a: 2, src: "lecture_02.py, tensors_memory",
        expl: "float16 halves memory but has poor dynamic range: 1e-8 underflows to 0, which can make training unstable. bfloat16, from Google Brain in 2018, keeps 2 bytes but has float32's dynamic range, so 1e-8 survives; its worse resolution matters less for deep learning." },
      { q: "The lecture totals training at 6 × (data points) × (parameters) FLOPs. Where does the 6 come from?", opts: ["Two for the forward pass, and four for the optimizer's state update", "Six bytes of state per parameter: weights, gradients, Adam moments", "Each parameter takes part in six matrix multiplications per layer", "Forward is 2 per point per parameter; backward is twice that, 4"], a: 3, src: "lecture_02.py, tensor_operations_flops and gradients_flops",
        expl: "A matmul does one multiply and one add per (i, j, k): 2 × points × parameters forward. Backward needs, for each weight matrix, one matmul for the weight's gradient and one for its input's gradient — 4 × points × parameters — so 6 in all." },
      { q: "Why does the lecture divide randomly initialised weights by sqrt(input_dim)?", opts: ["Otherwise each output grows like sqrt(input_dim), destabilising training", "It makes every weight positive, so that ReLU never outputs exactly zero", "It turns the normal distribution into a uniform one from -1 to 1", "It cancels out the learning rate, so one rate suits every layer"], a: 0, src: "lecture_02.py, module_parameters",
        expl: "With unit-variance weights each output of x @ w sums input_dim terms and scales as sqrt(input_dim); large values can blow up gradients. Rescaling by 1/sqrt(input_dim) makes outputs invariant to the width — Xavier initialisation, up to a constant — and truncating the normal at ±3 rules out outliers." },
      { q: "What concrete mixed-precision plan does the lecture give?", opts: ["bfloat16 for parameters and gradients, float32 only for the loss", "bfloat16 or fp8 for the forward pass's activations, float32 for the rest", "fp8 everywhere, keeping float32 only for the final saved checkpoint", "float16 for the even-numbered layers and float32 for the odd-numbered ones"], a: 1, src: "lecture_02.py, mixed_precision_training",
        expl: "Higher precision is more accurate and stable but costs memory and compute; lower precision the reverse. The plan: float32 by default, with bfloat16 or fp8 for the forward pass (activations) and float32 for parameters and gradients. PyTorch's AMP library automates this." },
      { q: "The lecture's napkin math: 70B parameters, 15T tokens, 6 FLOPs per token per parameter, on 1024 H100s at 989.5 TFLOP/s each with MFU 0.5. About how many days?", num: 144, tol: 1, src: "lecture_02.py, motivating_questions",
        expl: "Total: 6 × 70e9 × 15e12 = 6.3e24 FLOPs. Per day: 989.5e12 × 0.5 × 1024 × 86,400 ≈ 4.38e22. 6.3e24 / 4.38e22 ≈ 144 days — the 989.5 being half the 1979 TFLOP/s headline, which assumes sparsity." },
      { q: "How large a model, in billions of parameters, fits naively on 8 H100s of 80 GB each with AdamW in float32: 4 bytes for parameters, 4 for gradients and 8 for optimizer state, activations ignored?", num: 40, src: "lecture_02.py, motivating_questions",
        expl: "16 bytes per parameter; 8 × 80e9 = 640e9 bytes; 640e9 / 16 = 40e9 parameters. The lecture's caveat: activations, which depend on batch size and sequence length, are not counted." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.2": {
    takeaway: "Modern LLMs differ less than their papers suggest: nearly all use pre-norm, most use RMSNorm, gated activations, rotary position embeddings, no bias terms, d_ff ≈ 4 d_model (8/3 for gated units), heads × head dim = d_model, and weight decay rather than dropout. Where they differ, stability and inference cost — not raw FLOPs — usually decide.",
    beats: [
      { t: "From the original Transformer to today's", d: "The 2017 Transformer used sine position embeddings, ReLU feed-forward layers and post-norm LayerNorm. The course's variant puts the norm in front of each block, uses RoPE and SwiGLU, and drops the bias terms. Since then dozens of dense models have shipped, with low consensus except one thing — and a trend towards LLaMA-like designs." },
      { t: "Pre-norm, and RMSNorm", d: "Keep the norm off the residual path: almost everyone does, for stability and larger learning rates (Grok and Gemma 2 add a second norm outside the stream). RMSNorm drops LayerNorm's mean and bias; it barely changes FLOPs, but FLOPs are not runtime — normalisation is data movement, so fewer parameters to move saves wall-clock time. Bias terms are dropped for the same reasons." },
      { t: "Gated activations", d: "ReLU, GeLU, then gated units: multiply the activated xW1 entrywise by a second projection xV. SwiGLU and GeGLU give fairly consistent gains (Shazeer 2020) and are standard after 2023, though GPT-3 shows they aren't necessary. Some models also run attention and MLP in parallel to fuse matrix multiplies." },
      { t: "Rotary position embeddings", d: "We want attention scores to depend only on relative position. Sine embeddings leave cross-terms, absolute ones are absolute, and relative biases are not inner products. RoPE pairs coordinates and rotates them by position-dependent angles — multiplicative, no cross-terms — applied to queries and keys in every layer." },
      { t: "Hyperparameters with a consensus", d: "d_ff = 4 d_model almost always (about 8/3 for gated units; T5's 64× worked but its successor went back to 2.5). Heads × head dim = d_model in most models. Aspect ratio d_model/n_layer sits around 100-200, and depth costs parallelism and latency. Vocabularies: 30-50K monolingual, 100-250K multilingual." },
      { t: "Regularisation that isn't", d: "Pretraining sees trillions of tokens once, so overfitting is unlikely; newer models drop dropout and keep weight decay — not to control overfitting, but because it interacts with the learning rate schedule." },
      { t: "Stability tricks", d: "Softmaxes are where training blows up. The z-loss keeps the output softmax's normaliser near 1 (PaLM, OLMo 2); QK-norm normalises queries and keys before the attention softmax; logit soft-capping squashes logits with tanh." },
      { t: "Cheaper attention", d: "Decoding one token at a time re-reads the KV cache each step, so arithmetic intensity is poor. MQA shares one key/value head across all queries; GQA uses a few, with little or no perplexity cost. Sliding-window attention bounds the context per layer, and recent models interleave full-attention layers (e.g. every fourth) with windowed ones." },
    ],
    worked: "Sizing a gated FFN: a ReLU FFN with d_ff = 4 d_model holds 2 × 4 d_model² = 8 d_model² weights. A gated FFN has three d_model × d_ff matrices, so 3 d_ff d_model = 8 d_model² gives d_ff = 8/3 d_model ≈ 2.67 d_model — for d_model = 4096, about 10,900, which implementations round to a convenient multiple.",
    watch: "Judging an architecture change by FLOPs alone. RMSNorm and dropped biases save almost no arithmetic yet speed training up, because memory movement, not multiplication, is what those operations cost.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "What is the one architecture choice the lecture says nearly every modern LM agrees on?", opts: ["Rotary position embeddings, used by every model since GPT-3", "SwiGLU feed-forward layers, which replaced ReLU in every model", "Pre-norm: keep the LayerNorm off the main residual signal path", "Post-norm, as in the original Transformer, for stable gradients"], a: 2, src: "2025 Lecture 3 - architecture.pdf, slides 9-13",
        expl: "Almost all modern LMs put the norm in front of each block so it doesn't sit on the residual stream (BERT was post-norm; OPT-350M is an odd exception). The original claimed advantage was removing warmup; today it is stability and larger learning rates. Some recent models add a second norm outside the stream." },
      { q: "RMSNorm saves only a sliver of FLOPs. Why does the lecture say it still speeds models up?", opts: ["It removes warmup, so the same run needs fewer training steps", "It lets the model drop attention biases, which halves memory", "It cuts the FLOPs of every matrix multiply that follows it", "FLOPs are not runtime: moving fewer values saves wall-clock time"], a: 3, src: "2025 Lecture 3 - architecture.pdf, slides 14-19",
        expl: "Matrix multiplies are the vast majority of FLOPs, so dropping the mean and the bias saves almost no arithmetic. But normalisation is dominated by data movement — a low FLOP-to-memory ratio — so fewer operations and parameters to move do cut wall-clock time, and RMSNorm works as well as LayerNorm." },
      { q: "What property is RoPE designed to give the attention score between two tokens?", opts: ["A learned vector is added for each absolute position of a token", "It depends only on their relative position, by rotating q and k", "It is zero beyond a fixed window, so its cost grows only linearly", "It mixes absolute and relative terms, as sine embeddings do"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 3 - architecture.pdf, slides 30-35",
        expl: "RoPE wants the inner product of f(x, i) and f(y, j) to depend on x, y and i - j only. Inner products are invariant to rotation, so it pairs up coordinates and rotates each pair by a position-dependent angle — multiplying by sines and cosines rather than adding them, so there are no cross-terms — at every attention layer." },
      { q: "Why do MQA and GQA reduce the number of key/value heads?", opts: ["Decoding re-reads the KV cache every step; fewer KV heads, less to move", "Fewer heads improve attention quality by removing redundant heads", "It allows a larger training batch, since queries need no gradients", "It removes the softmax, which is the least stable part of the attention layer"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 3 - architecture.pdf, slides 58-64",
        expl: "In training, attention has high arithmetic intensity. In incremental decoding tokens come one at a time and the KV cache is read at every step, so arithmetic intensity is poor. MQA shares one key/value head across all query heads; GQA keeps a few — a knob between expressiveness and inference cost, with little perplexity cost." },
      { q: "A ReLU FFN with d_ff = 4 × d_model holds two d_model × d_ff matrices; a gated FFN such as SwiGLU holds three. What d_ff / d_model keeps the parameter count unchanged?", num: 2.67, tol: 0.01, src: "2025 Lecture 3 - architecture.pdf, slides 23 and 37-41",
        expl: "Two matrices of d_model × 4 d_model hold 8 d_model² weights; three of d_model × r d_model hold 3r d_model². 3r = 8 gives r = 8/3 ≈ 2.67 — the 2/3 scale-down the lecture describes, seen in LLaMA 70B (2.68) and Qwen 14B (2.67)." },
      { q: "T5 (11B) has 128 attention heads of dimension 128 and d_model = 1024. What is (number of heads × head dim) / d_model?", num: 16, src: "2025 Lecture 3 - architecture.pdf, slides 42-44",
        expl: "128 × 128 = 16,384, and 16,384 / 1024 = 16. Most models keep this ratio at 1 — GPT-3 has 96 × 128 = 12,288 = d_model — and T5 is a notable Google exception, as is its d_ff of 64 × d_model." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.3": {
    takeaway: "A mixture of experts swaps each feed-forward layer for many expert FFNs and a router that sends every token to only a few, so parameters grow while FLOPs per token stay put. The hard parts are the discrete routing decision, which can't be differentiated and is trained with heuristic balancing losses, and the instability and systems complexity that come with it.",
    beats: [
      { t: "What an MoE is", d: "Replace the big feed-forward layer with many FFNs and a selector layer. Each token uses only a few, so the number of experts can rise without changing FLOPs. At the same FLOPs, more parameters does better; MoEs train faster, compete with dense models, and parallelise across devices — most of the best open models are MoEs." },
      { t: "Why they took a while", d: "The infrastructure is complex and pays off mainly across many nodes, and the training objectives are heuristic and sometimes unstable. Most MoEs replace the MLP; MoE attention exists but is rare." },
      { t: "Routing: token-choice top-k", d: "Tokens can choose experts, experts can choose tokens, or routing can be solved globally — but almost everyone uses token-choice top-k: Switch k = 1, Mixtral and Grok 2, Qwen and DBRX 4, DeepSeek 7 or 8. Some routers softmax before choosing top-k; Mixtral, DBRX and DeepSeek v3 softmax after it. Hashing is a common baseline." },
      { t: "Fine-grained and shared experts", d: "DeepSeek and Qwen use many smaller experts plus a few shared experts that every token uses. DeepSeek's ablations favour both; OLMoE's find gains from fine-grained experts only. DeepSeek v3: 256 routed experts, 8 active, 1 shared." },
      { t: "Training a discrete choice", d: "Sparse gating is not differentiable. Reinforcement learning works but its variance and complexity keep it rare; stochastic perturbations were tried and dropped; in practice, heuristic balancing losses spread tokens evenly — the Switch loss downweights frequently used experts — and DeepSeek v3 adds per-expert biases learned online." },
      { t: "Systems, stability, fine-tuning", d: "Each expert can sit on its own device; libraries such as MegaBlocks use smarter sparse matmuls. Token dropping happens per batch, so other people's queries can drop your token. Keep the router in float32 with a z-loss. Sparse MoEs overfit small fine-tuning sets: fine-tune only the non-MoE MLPs, or use lots of data." },
      { t: "Upcycling and DeepSeek", d: "A pretrained dense model can initialise an MoE (MiniCPM, Qwen MoE). DeepSeek's line: v1 with shared and fine-grained experts and balancing losses; v2 adding communication balancing and top-M device routing; v3 with auxiliary-loss-free balancing, multi-head latent attention to shrink the KV cache, and multi-token prediction." },
    ],
    worked: "Routing one token with top-2 and softmax after top-k: router scores for four experts 2, 1, 0, -1; keep the two largest, 2 and 1; softmax over them gives e/(e + 1) ≈ 0.73 and 0.27. The token's output is 0.73 × expert 1's FFN output + 0.27 × expert 2's; the other experts are never run for it.",
    watch: "Counting an MoE's total parameters as its cost. Compute per token follows the active experts, not the total — while memory, communication and load balance follow everything.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "What does the lecture say an MoE layer replaces, and what does that buy?", opts: ["Attention heads with experts, which removes attention's quadratic cost", "The embedding table with experts, one for each language in the corpus", "The FFN, with many FFNs and a router: more parameters at the same FLOPs", "The whole model, with several small models whose outputs are averaged"], a: 2, src: "2025 Lecture 4 - MoEs.pdf, slides 3-13",
        expl: "Typically each MLP becomes many expert MLPs plus a selector; each token goes to only a few, so the number of experts — and parameters — grows without changing FLOPs per token. Same FLOPs, more parameters does better, and MoEs also train faster and parallelise across devices. MoE attention exists but is less common." },
      { q: "Sparse gating decisions aren't differentiable. Which fix does the lecture say people use in practice?", opts: ["Learning the routing policy by reinforcement, as REINFORCE does", "Adding Gaussian noise to the router so that gating becomes differentiable", "Solving a linear assignment problem for every batch of tokens", "Heuristic balancing losses that push tokens to spread across experts"], a: 3, src: "2025 Lecture 4 - MoEs.pdf, slides 24-31",
        expl: "RL is arguably the right solution, but its gradient variance and complexity keep it rare; stochastic perturbations (Shazeer 2017, Switch's jitter) were tried and later dropped. In practice: heuristic balancing losses — the Switch Transformer's penalises frequently used experts more strongly — or DeepSeek v3's per-expert biases." },
      { q: "Why might an MoE give a different answer to the same query at different times?", opts: ["Experts are resampled at random each time the model is loaded", "The router adds Gaussian noise to each routing decision it makes", "Tokens are dropped per batch, so others' queries can drop yours", "Each expert was trained on a different random subset of the data"], a: 2, src: "2025 Lecture 4 - MoEs.pdf, slide 34",
        expl: "Experts have limited capacity, and when too many tokens route to one, some are dropped — decided across the whole batch. Whether your token is processed then depends on what else is in the batch: other people's queries can drop your token, one speculation about GPT-4's non-determinism." },
      { q: "What stability fix does the lecture give for the MoE router?", opts: ["Dropping the router's softmax in favour of a hard argmax", "Freezing the router after warmup so its decisions stop changing", "Training the router at ten times the rest of the model's rate", "Computing the router in float32, sometimes with a z-loss"], a: 3, src: "2025 Lecture 4 - MoEs.pdf, slides 35-36",
        expl: "Routers rely on softmaxes over exponentials, which are fragile in low precision. Zoph et al. 2022 compute just the expert router in float32 and add an auxiliary z-loss on its logits; the lecture shows what happens to training without the z-loss." },
      { q: "With softmax applied after top-k (as in Mixtral), a token's router scores for four experts are 2, 1, 0 and -1, and k = 2. What gate weight does the top expert get? Three decimals.", num: 0.731, tol: 0.001, src: "2025 Lecture 4 - MoEs.pdf, slides 17-19",
        expl: "Top-2 keeps the scores 2 and 1; a softmax over just those gives e² / (e² + e¹) = e / (e + 1) ≈ 0.731, and 0.269 to the second. The other two experts get no weight and are not computed for this token." },
      { q: "DeepSeek v1's fine-grained experts are a quarter the size of a standard FFN, and each token uses 6 routed plus 2 shared experts. How many standard-FFN equivalents of expert compute does a token use?", num: 2, src: "2025 Lecture 4 - MoEs.pdf, slides 20-23 and 41",
        expl: "(6 + 2) × 1/4 = 2. Fine-grained experts split each FFN into smaller ones so a token can combine more specialists at the same cost, and shared experts are always on. DeepSeek's ablations favour both; OLMoE's find gains only from fine-grained experts." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.4": {
    takeaway: "GPUs win by running a huge number of identical threads, and matrix multiplies on tensor cores are far faster than anything else — but compute has outgrown memory, so speed comes from moving less data: lower precision, fused kernels, recomputation, coalesced reads and tiling through shared memory. FlashAttention is all of these applied to attention.",
    beats: [
      { t: "Why GPUs", d: "Language models improve predictably with compute, and since Dennard scaling ran out, the gains come from parallel hardware — GPU throughput has grown over 1000× in ten years. A CPU optimises for latency with a few fast threads; a GPU optimises for throughput with many tiny ALUs and little branching support." },
      { t: "Anatomy and execution model", d: "A GPU has many streaming multiprocessors (SMs), each running blocks of threads on its streaming processors. Threads run in warps of 32 executing the same instruction on different data (SIMT). Registers and shared memory live inside the SM; L2 is on the die; global DRAM is off-chip and far slower, and anything shared across blocks goes through it." },
      { t: "Matmuls are special, memory is the wall", d: "Tensor cores make matrix multiplies over 10× faster than other floating-point work, and FLOPs have scaled faster than memory bandwidth — it is hard to keep compute fed. The roofline model asks the key question: is a workload memory-bound or compute-bound?" },
      { t: "Tricks 1-3: precision, fusion, recomputation", d: "Fewer bits mean fewer bits to move, raising arithmetic intensity. Fusing operations keeps intermediates on-chip — sin²x + cos²x is five kernels naively, one fused, and torch.compile does such fusions. Recomputing cheap activations in the backward pass can beat storing them: three stacked sigmoids need 5/8 of the memory accesses." },
      { t: "Tricks 4-5: coalescing and tiling", d: "DRAM is read in bursts, so a warp's reads should fall in one burst. Tiling loads blocks of the input matrices into shared memory and reuses them, cutting global reads by the tile size — but tile sizes that don't divide the matrix, or misaligned rows, waste work." },
      { t: "The matrix mystery", d: "Matmul speed rises and dips with size because of alignment and wave quantization: with 256 × 128 tiles, a 1792 output needs 98 tiles but 1793 needs 120, more than an A100's 108 SMs, so a second mostly idle wave runs." },
      { t: "FlashAttention", d: "Attention is three matmuls with a softmax between. FlashAttention tiles the matmuls, fuses the exponential, and computes the softmax tile by tile with an online max and a telescoping sum, so the full score matrix never goes to global memory; the backward pass recomputes it tile by tile." },
    ],
    worked: "Arithmetic intensity of elementwise ReLU on n values: per value, one read and one write and one FLOP. In float32 that is 8 bytes per FLOP; in float16, 4. With intensity this low the operation is memory-bound — the fix is not a faster ALU but fewer bytes moved, by lower precision or by fusing the ReLU into the kernel that produced its input.",
    watch: "Counting FLOPs to predict speed. Most non-matmul operations are memory-bound, so their cost is the bytes they move — and a matrix whose size just exceeds a tile multiple can run slower than a larger one.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "How does the lecture contrast GPUs with CPUs?", opts: ["GPUs run very many threads for throughput; CPUs a few fast ones for latency", "GPUs have more cache per core and far stronger branch prediction than CPUs", "GPUs finish each single thread sooner; CPUs process more data in total", "GPUs run different instructions on each thread; CPUs run the same one"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 5 - GPUs.pdf, slides 8-12",
        expl: "A CPU optimises for latency — a few fast threads with plenty of control logic and cache. A GPU optimises for throughput: many tiny ALUs, much less support for branching, and within a warp all 32 threads execute the same instruction on different data (SIMT)." },
      { q: "What does operator fusion save?", opts: ["FLOPs, by merging several matrix multiplies into a larger one", "Trips to memory: intermediates stay on-chip, not written back", "Precision, by keeping every intermediate result in float32", "Compile time, by caching each kernel after its first launch"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 5 - GPUs.pdf, slides 26-29",
        expl: "Picture the GPU as a factory and memory as a distant warehouse: shipping every intermediate back and forth is wasteful. sin²x + cos²x launches 5 kernels naively but can be one fused kernel, and compilers such as torch.compile do easy fusions like this automatically." },
      { q: "Why can recomputing activations in the backward pass beat storing them?", opts: ["Re-reading stored activations costs memory traffic; recomputing is cheaper", "Stored activations lose precision, so recomputed ones are more accurate", "The backward pass needs different activations from the forward pass", "Recomputation lets the GPU skip the forward pass of the next batch"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 5 - GPUs.pdf, slides 30-32",
        expl: "For low-arithmetic-intensity operations such as stacked sigmoids, writing activations out and reading them back dominates — 8 memory reads and writes for three sigmoids. Discarding them and recomputing from the input needs 5/8 of the accesses: trading compute, which is plentiful, for memory traffic, which is not." },
      { q: "How does FlashAttention compute exact attention without writing the full score matrix to memory?", opts: ["It approximates softmax with a low-rank sketch of the score matrix", "Tiles of QKᵀ with an online softmax: a running max, telescoping sum", "It drops attention to distant tokens, keeping a sliding window only", "It stores the score matrix in float8, small enough to fit in SRAM"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 5 - GPUs.pdf, slides 46-50",
        expl: "It tiles the Q, K, V matrix multiplies through shared memory, fuses the exponential, and computes the softmax tile by tile with the online trick — keep a running maximum and rescale a telescoping sum — so the n × n scores never reach global memory. The backward pass recomputes them tile by tile." },
      { q: "With 256 × 128 tiles, a 1793 × 1793 output needs 8 × 15 = 120 tiles, more than an A100's 108 SMs. How many tiles does a 1600 × 1600 output need?", num: 91, src: "2025 Lecture 5 - GPUs.pdf, slides 41-44",
        expl: "ceil(1600 / 256) = 7 (6.25 rounded up) and ceil(1600 / 128) = 13 (12.5 rounded up): 7 × 13 = 91 tiles, one wave on 108 SMs. At 1793 the count jumps to 120 and a second, mostly idle wave is needed — wave quantization." },
      { q: "Elementwise ReLU in float16 reads each value once and writes it once, for one FLOP. How many bytes move per FLOP?", num: 4, src: "2025 Lecture 5 - GPUs.pdf, slides 23-24",
        expl: "A float16 value is 2 bytes: 2 read + 2 written = 4 bytes per FLOP, against 8 in float32. Fewer bits mean fewer bits to move, so low precision raises arithmetic intensity — which is what limits memory-bound operations like this one." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.5": {
    takeaway: "Measure before optimising: benchmark for end-to-end time (with warmup and synchronisation), profile to see which kernels actually run. Then the principle is the same at every level — PyTorch, CUDA, Triton or torch.compile — organise the computation to minimise reads and writes, which for elementwise and row-wise operations means fusing them into one kernel.",
    beats: [
      { t: "The execution model, again", d: "An A100 has 108 SMs, 80GB of DRAM, a 40MB L2 and fast per-SM memory. Threads run f(i); thread blocks are scheduled on one SM and share its memory; a grid is all the blocks. Blocks run in waves, so the last wave can leave SMs idle — aim for at least 4× as many blocks as SMs. Matmuls are compute-bound; nearly everything else is memory-bound." },
      { t: "Benchmark, then profile", d: "Spec sheets and papers don't know your library, hardware and workload. benchmark() warms up, calls torch.cuda.synchronize() because CUDA launches are asynchronous, and averages several trials. The PyTorch profiler then shows which CUDA kernels ran — different ones for different sizes, named after their library and tile size — and flame graphs show where an MLP's time goes." },
      { t: "Why fusion matters", d: "Each operation reads from DRAM, computes and writes back — the warehouse and the factory. manual_gelu, the tanh formula in PyTorch ops, launches a kernel per operation; torch's gelu is one fused kernel, and significantly faster." },
      { t: "Writing it in CUDA", d: "A CUDA kernel is the code one thread runs, using blockIdx, blockDim and threadIdx to find its element, with a bounds check for the last block. load_inline compiles it from Python. The hand-written GeLU beats manual_gelu but not PyTorch." },
      { t: "Writing it in Triton", d: "Triton, from OpenAI in 2021, is Python and thinks in blocks, not threads: it handles coalescing, shared memory and scheduling within an SM, while scheduling across SMs stays manual. The Triton GeLU loads a block with a mask, computes, and stores; its PTX shows one thread handling 8 elements (thread coarsening)." },
      { t: "Or don't write a kernel", d: "torch.compile turns manual_gelu into a generated Triton kernel without hand-written CUDA or Triton. Compilers will keep getting better; hand-written kernels pay off for what they can't fuse." },
      { t: "Softmax: a row-wise operation", d: "The naive softmax makes five passes — max, subtract, exp, sum, divide — for 5MN + M reads and 3MN + 2M writes, where MN of each would do. The Triton version gives each row its own block, loads it once (padding with minus infinity), computes in registers and writes once." },
    ],
    worked: "Choosing a Triton grid: for n elements and BLOCK_SIZE = 1024, launch triton.cdiv(n, 1024) programs. Program pid handles offsets pid × 1024 + arange(1024), with mask = offsets < n so the last block doesn't read or write out of bounds. For a 16384 × 16384 tensor that is 262,144 programs — far more than 4× the 108 SMs.",
    watch: "Timing GPU code without torch.cuda.synchronize(): the clock measures how fast Python queued the kernel, not how long it ran. And trusting the first run, which includes compilation and cold caches.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Why does the lecture's benchmark() call torch.cuda.synchronize() before stopping the clock?", opts: ["To flush the L2 cache, so that every trial starts with a cold cache", "To force float32, since bfloat16 timings are not reproducible", "CUDA launches are asynchronous; without it you'd time the launch", "To copy the result back to the CPU so the timing includes it"], a: 2, src: "lecture_06.py, benchmarking",
        expl: "The CPU queues GPU kernels and moves on, so a timer stopped right after run() measures little more than the launch. benchmark() also does warmup runs first — first calls can be slower from compilation and uncached state — and averages several trials to capture variance." },
      { q: "manual_gelu writes the tanh form of GeLU in PyTorch operations; the lecture finds torch's own gelu significantly faster. Why?", opts: ["The built-in uses the exact formula, which needs fewer arithmetic steps", "manual_gelu runs on the CPU, since plain Python arithmetic can't use a GPU", "The built-in replaces tanh with a lookup table held in on-chip memory", "Each manual op is its own kernel and DRAM round trip; the built-in is one"], a: 3, src: "lecture_06.py, kernel_fusion_motivation",
        expl: "Every elementwise operation in manual_gelu launches a kernel that reads its input from global memory and writes its result back. torch's gelu (called with the same tanh approximation) does it all in one kernel — the warehouse-and-factory point. The lecture's own CUDA version also beats manual_gelu but not PyTorch's." },
      { q: "According to the lecture's table, what does Triton manage automatically that CUDA leaves to you?", opts: ["Everything, including scheduling thread blocks across the SMs", "Only memory coalescing; shared memory is still managed by hand", "Coalescing, shared-memory management and scheduling within an SM", "Only scheduling across SMs; everything inside an SM stays manual"], a: 2, src: "lecture_06.py, triton_introduction and print_ptx_main",
        expl: "Triton is written in Python and thinks in thread blocks rather than threads; its compiler handles coalesced DRAM transfers, shared memory and scheduling within each SM, while scheduling across SMs stays manual. Working on blocks enables tricks such as thread coarsening — one thread processing 8 elements." },
      { q: "What general rule about arithmetic intensity (FLOPs per byte) does the lecture give?", opts: ["Everything on a GPU is compute-bound once the batch is large enough", "Elementwise ops are compute-bound, and matrix multiplies memory-bound", "Intensity depends only on precision, not on which operation runs", "Matrix multiplication is compute-bound; nearly all else is memory-bound"], a: 3, src: "lecture_06.py, review_of_gpus",
        expl: "High intensity means compute-bound (good), low means memory-bound (bad). Matmuls reuse each loaded value many times; elementwise and reduction operations touch each value once or twice — which is why fusing them, so data is read and written once, pays off." },
      { q: "triton_gelu splits its input into blocks of BLOCK_SIZE = 1024 elements. How many blocks does it launch for a 16384 × 16384 tensor? (digits only, no commas)", num: 262144, src: "lecture_06.py, triton_gelu",
        expl: "16384 × 16384 = 268,435,456 elements, divided by 1024 = 262,144 blocks (triton.cdiv rounds up, but here it divides exactly). Each program masks with offsets < num_elements so a ragged last block stays in bounds." },
      { q: "manual_softmax's comments count reads per step for an M × N input: max MN, subtract MN + M, exp MN, sum MN, normalise MN. How many reads for the lecture's 2 × 3 example?", num: 32, src: "lecture_06.py, manual_softmax",
        expl: "5MN + M = 5 × 6 + 2 = 32 reads, alongside 3MN + 2M = 22 writes. A fused kernel reads and writes each value once — MN of each — which is why the comment expects about a 4× speedup." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.6": {
    takeaway: "No single GPU holds a large model or trains it fast enough, so training is split three ways: data parallelism (with ZeRO sharding its memory away), pipeline parallelism across layers, and tensor plus sequence parallelism within layers. Each spends a different scarce resource — memory, bandwidth or batch size — so real runs combine them by rule of thumb.",
    beats: [
      { t: "Why parallelise, and the collectives", d: "Single-GPU compute and memory both run out: most large models don't fit on one device. Work is split across GPUs within a node (fast interconnects) and across nodes (slower). The building blocks are collectives — all-reduce, broadcast, reduce, all-gather, reduce-scatter — and an all-reduce can be done as a reduce-scatter plus an all-gather, optimal when bandwidth-limited." },
      { t: "Data parallelism and its memory problem", d: "Split the batch across M machines and all-reduce the gradients: compute scales, communication is 2 × #params per batch, memory doesn't scale at all. Mixed-precision Adam needs about 16 bytes per parameter — bf16 weights and gradients, float32 master weights, two Adam moments — copied on every GPU." },
      { t: "ZeRO stages 1-3", d: "Stage 1 shards the optimizer state: reduce-scatter gradients, each worker updates its slice, all-gather parameters — the same traffic as DDP, so it's free. Stage 2 also shards gradients, sending each layer's gradients as soon as they're computed. Stage 3 (FSDP) shards parameters too, fetching them on demand and overlapping communication with compute, for 1.5× the traffic." },
      { t: "Pipeline parallelism", d: "Cutting the model by layers leaves each of n GPUs busy 1/n of the time. Micro-batches fill the pipeline, leaving a bubble of (stages - 1)/(micro-batches), so pipelines need big batches. They save memory and send only activations, point to point, so they suit slower inter-node links; zero-bubble schedules split the backward pass to fill the gaps." },
      { t: "Tensor and sequence parallelism", d: "Split matrix multiplies by columns and rows across GPUs, with all-reduces in the forward and backward passes: no bubble and no batch-size requirement, but heavy communication, so keep it within a node. Activations still grow, so sequence parallelism splits the pointwise LayerNorm and dropout work along the sequence. Context and expert parallelism split long sequences and experts." },
      { t: "Putting it together", d: "Until the model fits, use tensor parallelism up to the GPUs in a machine and pipeline parallelism across machines (or ZeRO-3); then scale with data parallelism, accumulating gradients if the batch is small. Tensor parallel 8 is often optimal; activation recomputation can pay for itself by allowing larger batches." },
      { t: "What recent models do", d: "OLMo's 7B used FSDP; DeepSeek uses ZeRO stage 1 with tensor, sequence and pipeline parallelism (v3: pipeline 16, expert 64-way); Yi uses ZeRO-1 with tensor and pipeline; Gemma 2 uses ZeRO-3 with model and data parallelism; Llama 3 405B notes how often GPUs fail at that scale." },
    ],
    worked: "Will it fit? With bf16 everything except float32 master weights, training costs 12 bytes per parameter. On 8 × 80 GB: unsharded, 80 / 12 ≈ 6.7B parameters. ZeRO-1 keeps 2 + 2 bytes and an eighth of the 8 optimizer bytes: 5 bytes, 16B. ZeRO-2 keeps 2 bytes and an eighth of 10: 3.25 bytes, 24.6B. ZeRO-3 keeps an eighth of all 12: 1.5 bytes, 53.3B.",
    watch: "Running tensor parallelism across nodes: its all-reduces in every layer need a fast interconnect, and on slow links it stalls the whole model. And counting only parameter memory — activations grow with batch and sequence length and need their own split.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Why does the lecture call ZeRO stage 1 'free'?", opts: ["Its reduce-scatter plus all-gather costs DDP's all-reduce: 2 × params", "It needs no communication, as each GPU updates every parameter itself", "It shards the activations, which are training's biggest memory cost", "It cuts the batch size each GPU needs, which shortens every single step"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 7 - Parallelism basics.pdf, slides 8 and 15-18",
        expl: "An all-reduce can be done as a reduce-scatter then an all-gather — the best you can do when bandwidth-limited. Stage 1 splits the optimizer state: reduce-scatter the gradients, each worker updates its slice, all-gather the parameters. Same 2 × #params traffic as DDP, with memory falling from (4 + K) to (4 + K/N) bytes per parameter." },
      { q: "What rule of thumb does the lecture give for combining the three kinds of parallelism?", opts: ["Pipeline within a machine, tensor parallel across machines, then data", "Tensor parallel within a machine, pipeline across machines, then data", "Data parallel first until the batch runs out, then tensor, then pipeline", "Use just one kind, since combining them multiplies communication costs"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 7 - Parallelism basics.pdf, slides 38-39 and 49-52",
        expl: "Until the model fits: tensor parallel up to the GPUs in one machine — it needs the fast intra-node interconnect, and 8 is often optimal — and pipeline parallel across machines, since only activations cross the slower links point to point (or ZeRO-3, depending on bandwidth). Then data parallel the rest of the way, accumulating gradients if the batch is small." },
      { q: "What does sequence parallelism split, and why?", opts: ["LayerNorm and dropout along the sequence, so those activations scale", "The attention heads across GPUs, so that each GPU computes a few heads", "The vocabulary across GPUs, so the output softmax fits on each device", "The optimizer state across nodes, so long sequences need less memory"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 7 - Parallelism basics.pdf, slides 40-45",
        expl: "Tensor parallelism splits the attention and MLP matmuls, but about 10sbh of activations per layer — LayerNorm, dropout and the block inputs — stay whole on every GPU and keep growing. Those operations are pointwise over the sequence, so splitting them along it (all-gather in, reduce-scatter out) makes activation memory scale linearly too." },
      { q: "Model parallelism and ZeRO stage 3 both split the parameters across GPUs. What does each communicate?", opts: ["Both send gradients only, never activations or the weights", "Model parallelism sends activations; ZeRO-3 sends parameters", "Model parallelism sends parameters; ZeRO-3 only activations", "Both send the full model to every GPU before each step"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 7 - Parallelism basics.pdf, slides 21-24 and 28",
        expl: "ZeRO-3 (FSDP) requests each block's parameters when needed and frees them afterwards, overlapping the all-gathers with compute — about 3 × #params of traffic per step. Pipeline and tensor parallelism keep their slice of the parameters in place and pass activations (and their gradients) instead." },
      { q: "With bf16 everything except float32 master weights — 12 bytes per parameter: 2 weights, 2 gradients, 8 master weights and Adam state — how many billion parameters fit on 8 × 80 GB GPUs under ZeRO stage 2? One decimal.", num: 24.6, tol: 0.1, src: "2025 Lecture 7 - Parallelism basics.pdf, slides 19-20 and 25",
        expl: "Each GPU keeps all the bf16 weights (2 bytes) and an eighth of the gradients and optimizer state: (2 + 8) / 8 = 1.25 bytes. 2 + 1.25 = 3.25 bytes per parameter, and 80e9 / 3.25 ≈ 24.6e9. Unsharded: 80/12 ≈ 6.7B; stage 1 (5 bytes) 16B; stage 3 (12/8) 53.3B." },
      { q: "A pipeline has 4 stages and processes 12 micro-batches per batch. What is the lecture's ratio of bubble time to useful compute, (stages - 1)/(micro-batches)?", num: 0.25, src: "2025 Lecture 7 - Parallelism basics.pdf, slides 30-33",
        expl: "(4 - 1) / 12 = 0.25: a quarter as much idle time as useful work. More micro-batches shrink the bubble, which is why pipelines need big batches; plain layer-wise parallelism leaves each of n GPUs busy only 1/n of the time." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.7": {
    takeaway: "Multi-GPU training rests on a handful of collective operations — broadcast, scatter, gather, reduce, all-gather, reduce-scatter, all-reduce — that NCCL turns into packets over NVLink and NVSwitch. With them, data, tensor and pipeline parallelism each fit in a few dozen lines of PyTorch; what separates them is which tensors cross the wire and how often.",
    beats: [
      { t: "The same theme, one level up", d: "Inside a GPU, fusion and tiling cut memory accesses; across GPUs, replication and sharding cut communication. The hierarchy runs from shared memory and HBM (3.9 TB/s on an H100) to NVLink within a node (900 GB/s, 18 links) to NVSwitch across nodes — far faster than the PCIe and Ethernet of a home machine." },
      { t: "Collective operations", d: "World size is the number of devices; each is a rank. Broadcast and scatter send out, gather collects, reduce combines with an associative op, 'all' means every rank gets the result, and all-reduce = reduce-scatter + all-gather. The recorded demo shows it: ranks holding [0, 1, 2, 3] + r all end with [6, 10, 14, 18]." },
      { t: "NCCL and torch.distributed", d: "NCCL detects the topology, picks the paths and launches CUDA kernels to move data; torch.distributed wraps it (nccl for GPUs, gloo for CPUs) behind calls such as all_gather_into_tensor. Benchmarked on one node, a 400 MB all-reduce took about 2 ms — roughly 275 GB/s effective." },
      { t: "Data parallelism in code", d: "Each rank takes batch/world_size examples, runs forward and backward, and all-reduces every gradient with AVG — the only difference from ordinary training. Losses differ across ranks, gradients and parameters don't." },
      { t: "Tensor parallelism in code", d: "Each rank holds a num_dim × num_dim/world_size slice of every layer, computes its share of the outputs, then all-gathers and concatenates the activations to full width before the next layer — a collective in every layer." },
      { t: "Pipeline parallelism in code", d: "Each rank holds a subset of the layers. Rank 0 splits the batch into micro-batches; each rank receives activations from the previous rank, applies its layers and sends the result on, point to point. Overlapping communication with computation to remove the bubble is left out." },
      { t: "What's missing", d: "Attention and other layers, overlap of communication and compute, and the bookkeeping that real systems need — or, in JAX on TPUs, declaring a sharding and letting the compiler do it. The summary: parallelise over batch, width, depth or length, and choose between recomputing, storing, or fetching from another GPU." },
    ],
    worked: "Reduce-scatter then all-gather on 4 ranks holding [0, 1, 2, 3] + r: summing element-wise gives [6, 10, 14, 18]; reduce-scatter leaves rank i with element i (6, 10, 14, 18); all-gather sends each rank everyone's element, so every rank ends with [6, 10, 14, 18] — exactly what one all-reduce produces.",
    watch: "Forgetting that every collective is a synchronisation point: one slow rank, or a rank that never calls the collective, stalls all the others. The demos call dist.barrier() before printing and torch.cuda.synchronize() before stopping a timer for this reason.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "In the lecture's bare-bones data parallelism, what is the one step added to ordinary training?", opts: ["Each rank keeps its own parameters, never synchronised with the others", "Rank 0 computes the whole batch's loss and broadcasts it to the rest", "After backward, every gradient is all-reduced (averaged) across ranks", "Activations are all-gathered after each layer so all ranks see them"], a: 2, src: "lecture_08.py, data_parallelism_main",
        expl: "Each rank takes its slice of the batch (128 / 4 = 32 examples), runs forward and backward, then all-reduces each parameter's gradient with AVG. Losses differ across ranks — they come from different data — but gradients, and so the parameters after the AdamW step, stay identical." },
      { q: "In the tensor-parallel demo, what happens after each layer's slice of the matmul?", opts: ["Gradients are reduce-scattered, so each rank updates only its slice", "Activations go point to point to the rank holding the next layer", "Each rank discards the others' outputs and keeps its own columns", "Activations are all-gathered and concatenated back to full width"], a: 3, src: "lecture_08.py, tensor_parallelism_main",
        expl: "Each rank holds a num_dim × num_dim/world_size slice of every layer (1024 × 256 on 4 ranks), so x @ params[i] yields only its share of the output columns. An all-gather collects every rank's batch × 256 block and torch.cat rebuilds batch × 1024 — a collective in every layer, which is why tensor parallelism wants fast links." },
      { q: "How does the pipeline-parallel demo move data between ranks?", opts: ["All ranks all-reduce the activations once each micro-batch is done", "Rank 0 broadcasts the batch, and every rank computes all the layers", "Rank 0 splits the batch into micro-batches; ranks send and recv them", "Each rank all-gathers the other ranks' parameters before every layer"], a: 2, src: "lecture_08.py, pipeline_parallelism_main",
        expl: "Each rank holds a subset of the layers (4 layers over 2 ranks). Rank 0 chunks the batch into micro-batches; each later rank recv()s a micro-batch's activations from the rank before, applies its layers and send()s the result on — point to point. Communication and compute are not overlapped, so the bubble remains." },
      { q: "What unifying theme does the lecture draw between last week's kernels and this week's multi-GPU training?", opts: ["Compute is cheap only in float32, so precision decides every design", "Distributed training needs no code beyond the standard PyTorch calls", "Every speed-up comes from adding more GPUs rather than from changing code", "Arrange the computation to avoid data-transfer bottlenecks, at every level"], a: 3, src: "lecture_08.py, main",
        expl: "In both cases compute sits far from the data. Inside a GPU the answer was fusion and tiling to reduce memory accesses; across GPUs it is replication and sharding to reduce communication — up a hierarchy from shared memory to HBM to NVLink (900 GB/s per H100, against 3.9 TB/s of HBM) to NVSwitch across nodes." },
      { q: "In the collective demo, rank r of 4 starts with the tensor [0, 1, 2, 3] + r. After a SUM reduce-scatter, what single value does rank 2 hold?", num: 14, src: "lecture_08.py, collective_operations_main; var/traces/lecture_08_stdout.txt",
        expl: "Reduce-scatter sums element-wise across ranks — [6, 10, 14, 18] — and gives rank i element i, so rank 2 gets 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 14. An all-gather of the four values then reproduces the all-reduce result on every rank: all-reduce = reduce-scatter + all-gather." },
      { q: "The all-reduce benchmark sends 100 × 1024² float32 elements on 4 ranks and counts sent bytes as size × 2 × (world_size - 1). How many GiB is that? Two decimals.", num: 2.34, tol: 0.01, src: "lecture_08.py, all_reduce",
        expl: "Size: 104,857,600 × 4 bytes = 400 MiB = 0.390625 GiB; × 2 (send input, receive output) × 3 = 2.34 GiB. Divided by world_size × duration, the recorded run measured about 275 GB/s." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.8": {
    takeaway: "Loss falls as a power law — a straight line on a log-log plot — in data, parameters and compute, which turns design into prediction: fit small models, extrapolate to the large one. That lets you choose architectures, optimizers and the split between model size and data before spending the big budget — while remembering that compute-optimal training is not deployment-optimal.",
    beats: [
      { t: "The question", d: "Given ten thousand H100s for a month, which model do you train? Tuning hyperparameters on big models is unaffordable, and cargo-culting existing LMs doesn't say how they were chosen. Scaling laws offer simple, predictive rules: tune on small models and extrapolate." },
      { t: "A short history", d: "Theory gave sample-complexity bounds; 1990s and 2000s work found log-linear data scaling; Hestness et al. (2017) showed predictable power laws across translation, language modelling and speech — even anticipating emergence and compute scaling. Kaplan et al. (2020) found such laws for many factors in language models." },
      { t: "Why power laws?", d: "Estimation error decays polynomially. Estimating a mean from n samples gives expected squared error σ²/n: log error falls with slope -1. Flexible nonparametric learning in d dimensions gives n^(-1/d) — much shallower — and one argument ties neural slopes to the data's intrinsic dimension." },
      { t: "Data scaling, beyond size", d: "Changing data composition shifts the offset, not the slope. Repeated data is worth less than fresh data, so effective data saturates with repetition, and data selection should adapt to scale." },
      { t: "Engineering with scaling laws", d: "Instead of training an LSTM GPT-3, compare the scaling curves of LSTMs and Transformers, or Adam and SGD. Depth matters little past a couple of layers, embedding parameters behave differently, critical batch size grows as the loss target falls, and optimal learning rates shift with scale unless parametrised (muP). Downstream tasks scale less predictably." },
      { t: "Model size versus data", d: "Joint laws such as error = n^(-α) + m^(-β) + C fit well and let you trade data against model size. Chinchilla found Kaplan's fits off — mainly from not accounting for learning-rate schedules — and gave three methods: minimum over training curves, IsoFLOP profiles, and a joint fit, the last later found flawed." },
      { t: "Train-optimal isn't deploy-optimal", d: "Chinchilla's roughly 20 tokens per parameter minimises training compute, but most deployed compute is inference, so models are over-trained: GPT-3 used 2 tokens per parameter, Llama 2 70B 29, Mistral 7B 110, Llama 3 70B 215." },
    ],
    worked: "An IsoFLOP analysis: choose several compute budgets C; for each, train models of several sizes N with D = C/(6N) tokens; plot final loss against N — each curve is U-shaped — and take its minimum. Fit a power law through the minima, N*(C), and extrapolate it to the budget you can actually afford.",
    watch: "Extrapolating a scaling law fitted with a fixed learning-rate schedule, or reading downstream benchmark scores as if they were as predictable as loss. Both errors have produced confident, wrong forecasts.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "What does Chinchilla's IsoFLOP method (method 2) do?", opts: ["Fixes several compute budgets, varies model size in each, fits the minima", "Trains one huge model and reads the optimum off its learning-rate schedule", "Fits one least-squares formula to every run on a model-size and data grid", "Takes the lowest point over the union of all the training curves it has"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 9 - Scaling laws basics.pdf, slides 45-49",
        expl: "For each FLOP budget, vary the parameter count (and so the tokens) and find the bottom of the U-shaped loss curve; the minima across budgets form a power law for the compute-optimal size. Method 1 takes the minimum over all training curves; method 3 fits a joint law by least squares — and was likely flawed, as a later re-fit of the recovered data found." },
      { q: "According to the lecture, how does changing the composition of the training data change a data scaling law?", opts: ["It changes the slope, so better data makes loss fall faster with size", "It shifts the offset, while the slope stays roughly as it was", "It turns the power law into an exponential decay in dataset size", "It has no effect at all, since only the number of tokens matters"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 9 - Scaling laws basics.pdf, slides 21-24",
        expl: "Data composition affects the offset, not the slope: a better or more relevant mix moves the whole log-log line down without changing how fast it falls. Distribution-shift scaling laws built on this show the value of diverse data; repetition, separately, makes extra tokens worth less." },
      { q: "Chinchilla's rule is about 20 tokens per parameter, yet Llama 3 70B used 215. Why does the lecture say that can make sense?", opts: ["Deployment compute is mostly inference, so a small model trained longer pays", "Chinchilla's fits were wrong, and 200 tokens per parameter is optimal", "Larger vocabularies need more tokens for each parameter to be learned", "More tokens per parameter cut the training FLOPs needed for a given loss"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 9 - Scaling laws basics.pdf, slide 50",
        expl: "Chinchilla answers which model is best for a fixed training budget. Serving cost grows with model size and usage, so the more a model will be used, the more it's worth paying upfront to train a smaller one far past compute-optimal — GPT-3 used 2 tokens per parameter, Mistral 7B 110, Llama 3 70B 215." },
      { q: "What design procedure do scaling laws make possible, in the lecture's words?", opts: ["Tune every hyperparameter on the full-size model, then shrink it", "Train a few small models, fit a scaling law, pick by its prediction", "Copy all the hyperparameters of the latest large open model as is", "Grow one model gradually, re-tuning at each doubling of its size"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 9 - Scaling laws basics.pdf, slides 26-39",
        expl: "Rather than the old, unpleasant way — tuning on big models — or cargo-culting existing LMs: train a few smaller models, establish a scaling law (Adam against SGD, Transformers against LSTMs, depth), and choose by its prediction. Caution: downstream task performance often scales much less predictably than loss." },
      { q: "Estimating a mean from n samples of N(μ, σ²) has expected squared error σ²/n — the lecture's toy scaling law. With σ² = 4, how many samples bring the expected squared error to 0.01?", num: 400, src: "2025 Lecture 9 - Scaling laws basics.pdf, slides 16-19",
        expl: "σ²/n = 0.01 gives n = 4 / 0.01 = 400. In logs, log error = -log n + 2 log σ: a slope of -1. Neural scaling laws have much shallower slopes, which the lecture relates to nonparametric rates such as n^(-1/d)." },
      { q: "Slide 50 lists Mistral 7B at 110 tokens per parameter, against Chinchilla's 20. Training compute is proportional to N × D. With the same compute as Mistral 7B's run, how many billion parameters would a Chinchilla-optimal model (20 tokens per parameter) have? One decimal.", num: 16.4, tol: 0.1, src: "2025 Lecture 9 - Scaling laws basics.pdf, slide 50",
        expl: "Mistral 7B: N × D = 7B × 770B. A model at 20 tokens per parameter with the same product has 20 N² = 7 × 770 (in units of 10^18), so N = √269.5 ≈ 16.4B, trained on about 328B tokens. Mistral chose a model under half that size and trained it far longer — over-training, which the slide justifies: most of a deployed model's compute is inference, so a smaller model is worth the extra upfront cost." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.9": {
    takeaway: "Inference is a different workload from training: prefill is compute-bound, but generation produces one token at a time and is memory-bound, dominated by reading the weights and the KV cache. So the levers are reading less — smaller KV caches, quantized weights, pruned or new architectures — checking cheaply instead of generating (speculative decoding), and managing memory for ragged, dynamic traffic (continuous batching, PagedAttention).",
    beats: [
      { t: "Why inference matters", d: "It runs in actual use, evaluation, test-time compute and RL rollouts, and unlike training it is paid every time. Metrics: time to first token, latency per token, throughput. Training sees all tokens at once; generation is sequential, so compute is hard to use fully." },
      { t: "Arithmetic intensity", d: "An X (B × D) @ W (D × F) matmul has intensity about B FLOPs per byte when B is small; an H100's ratio of FLOP/s to bandwidth is about 295, so the matmul is compute-limited only if B > 295. With a KV cache, the MLP's intensity is B × T, but attention's is S·T/(S + T): S/2 in prefill, below 1 in generation, and batching can't help because each sequence has its own cache." },
      { t: "Latency versus throughput", d: "Each generation step reads all parameters plus every sequence's KV cache. For Llama 2 13B on an H100: batch 1 gives about 8 ms per token and 125 tokens/s; batch 64, 24 ms and about 2,700 tokens/s; batch 256 doesn't fit and gains less. Copies of the model raise throughput at the same latency; small batches help time to first token." },
      { t: "Smaller KV caches", d: "GQA keeps N query heads but K key/value heads, shrinking the cache N/K-fold (MQA: K = 1). MLA projects keys and values down to a small latent (DeepSeek v2: 16,384 to 512, plus 64 for RoPE). Cross-layer attention shares KVs across layers; local attention keeps the cache independent of length, interleaved with some global layers." },
      { t: "Beyond the Transformer, and lossy shortcuts", d: "State-space models and linear attention replace the growing KV cache with a fixed state (Mamba, Jamba, BASED, MiniMax-01); diffusion models generate tokens in parallel. Quantization: bf16 by default, fp8, int8 (LLM.int8() keeps outliers in fp16), int4, with AWQ keeping the most activation-sensitive weights precise. Pruning removes unimportant layers or heads, then distils." },
      { t: "Speculative decoding", d: "A cheap draft model proposes a few tokens; the target scores them in parallel and accepts or rejects them by modified rejection sampling, so the output is an exact sample from the target. Better drafts (Medusa, EAGLE) mean more acceptances." },
      { t: "Dynamic workloads", d: "Requests arrive at different times with different lengths. Continuous batching schedules each decoding iteration, adding new requests as they come, and batches non-attention work across concatenated sequences. PagedAttention stores the KV cache in non-contiguous blocks, ending fragmentation and sharing prefixes with copy-on-write." },
    ],
    worked: "KV cache per sequence: tokens × (KV heads × head dim) × layers × 2 (key, value) × 2 bytes (bf16). Llama 2 13B at 1024 tokens: 1024 × 40 × 128 × 40 × 4 ≈ 0.84 GB. With 26 GB of weights, a batch of 64 needs about 80 GB; GQA with 8 KV heads cuts the cache 5×, which is what lets the batch grow to 256 and still fit.",
    watch: "Optimising generation's FLOPs. A decode step does little arithmetic per byte it reads, so the wins come from moving fewer bytes — the KV cache and the weights — not from faster multiplication.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Why does batching more requests not raise the arithmetic intensity of attention during generation, as it does for the MLP?", opts: ["Attention runs in float32, which halves the FLOPs per byte it reads", "Attention runs on the CPU, while the MLP layers run on the GPU", "Each sequence has its own KV cache, so no read is shared across it", "Attention has no matrix multiplies, only softmax and elementwise ops"], a: 2, src: "lecture_10.py, arithmetic_intensity_of_inference",
        expl: "MLP weights are the same for every sequence, so B concurrent requests reuse each weight read: intensity about B. In attention Q, K and V all depend on the sequence, so each request reads its own KV cache: intensity S·T/(S + T) — about S/2 in prefill but below 1 when generating one token (T = 1), whatever B is." },
      { q: "What guarantees that speculative decoding produces the same distribution as the target model?", opts: ["The draft is distilled until its outputs match the target's exactly", "Only tokens where draft and target agree on the argmax are kept", "The target model regenerates every token the draft model proposed", "Proposals are accepted or rejected by modified rejection sampling"], a: 3, src: "lecture_10.py, speculative_sampling",
        expl: "The draft p proposes a few tokens; the target q scores them in parallel — checking is cheaper than generating — and accepts each with probability min(1, q/p), resampling from the residual max(q - p, 0) on rejection. The result is an exact sample from q; a better draft just means more acceptances." },
      { q: "What problem does PagedAttention solve?", opts: ["Attention's quadratic cost, by skipping blocks of distant tokens", "Slow kernel launches, by fusing every layer into a single kernel", "KV-cache fragmentation, by storing it in non-contiguous blocks", "Precision loss in the KV cache, by keeping its pages in float32"], a: 2, src: "lecture_10.py, paged_attention",
        expl: "Reserving a contiguous KV region up to the maximum length wastes memory inside it (internal fragmentation) and between regions (external). vLLM's PagedAttention splits each sequence's cache into blocks, as an OS pages memory, and lets sequences share prefix blocks — a system prompt, several samples — with copy-on-write." },
      { q: "What trade-off does the lecture draw between batch size, latency and throughput in generation?", opts: ["Larger batches improve latency and throughput until memory runs out", "Smaller batches improve both, since each request gets the whole GPU", "Batch size changes neither, because generation is compute-bound", "Larger batches raise throughput but worsen latency; smaller, the reverse"], a: 3, src: "lecture_10.py, throughput_and_latency; var/traces/lecture_10.json",
        expl: "Generation is memory-bound: each step reads all the parameters plus every sequence's KV cache. Batching shares the parameter reads, so tokens per second rise — about 125 at batch 1 and 2,700 at 64 for Llama 2 13B on an H100 — but each step reads more cache, so latency grows from about 8 ms to 24 ms." },
      { q: "An H100 does 989 TFLOP/s against 3.35 TB/s of memory bandwidth. A bf16 X (B × D) @ W (D × F) matmul with B much smaller than D and F has intensity about B. Above roughly what B does it become compute-limited?", num: 295, tol: 1, src: "lecture_10.py, review_of_arithmetic_intensity",
        expl: "Accelerator intensity = 989e12 / 3.35e12 ≈ 295 FLOPs per byte. The matmul does 2BDF FLOPs over about 2DF bytes of weights, so its intensity is about B: compute-limited only when B > 295. Generation with one token per request is the B = 1 case — a matrix-vector product." },
      { q: "Llama 2 13B has 40 layers and 40 key/value heads of dimension 128. In bf16, how many GB (1e9 bytes) does the KV cache take for one 1024-token sequence? Two decimals.", num: 0.84, tol: 0.01, src: "lecture_10.py, compute_transformer_stats and llama2_13b_config",
        expl: "S × (K × H) × L × 2 (key and value) × 2 bytes = 1024 × 5120 × 40 × 4 = 838,860,800 bytes ≈ 0.84 GB. At batch 64 that is about 54 GB on top of 26 GB of weights; GQA with 8 KV heads cuts the cache 5×." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.10": {
    takeaway: "Published scaling recipes since Chinchilla share three moves: make hyperparameters transfer across scale (muP, or fitted laws for batch size and learning rate), make the Chinchilla sweep affordable (warmup-stable-decay schedules), and size the model by IsoFLOP or joint fits. muP mostly works — but not with everything a modern LM does.",
    beats: [
      { t: "The practical questions", d: "Does Chinchilla's approach work in practice? Can fitting scaling laws cost less compute? Should architectures be parametrised so they scale nicely? Three recent models published detailed recipes: Cerebras-GPT, MiniCPM and DeepSeek." },
      { t: "Cerebras-GPT and MiniCPM: muP", d: "Cerebras-GPT (0.1-13B, Chinchilla recipe) found scaling more predictable with muP. MiniCPM (1-2.5B, matching many 7B models) uses muP for initialisation, fixes the aspect ratio, and fits optimal batch size (growing polynomially as loss falls), learning rate and tokens per parameter directly — about 192, far beyond 20." },
      { t: "WSD makes the sweep cheap", d: "A Chinchilla analysis needs runs trained to completion at each data size, so its cost grows from n to n². Warmup-stable-decay holds the learning rate flat and decays only at the end (about 10%), so any stable-phase checkpoint can be decayed into a finished run. It matches cosine in final quality." },
      { t: "DeepSeek: no muP", d: "DeepSeek (7B and 67B) assumes most hyperparameters are scale-invariant, fits how optimal batch size and learning rate scale from near-optimal small runs, uses a WSD-style schedule with two 10% decay steps, and sizes the model with an IsoFLOP analysis — whose fits predict final losses well." },
      { t: "The newest, less detailed", d: "Llama 3 used IsoFLOPs (a 39:1 ratio) and scaled compute to downstream performance; Hunyuan-1 did IsoFLOPs for MoE (96 tokens per active parameter); MiniMax-01 used architecture scaling laws and Chinchilla method 1." },
      { t: "What muP is", d: "Two conditions as width grows: activations stay order 1 at initialisation, and change by order 1 after a gradient step. Working through a deep linear network gives width-dependent initialisation and per-layer learning rates — for Adam, scaled as 1/fan-in — where standard parametrisation keeps the rate fixed." },
      { t: "Does it transfer?", d: "In a width-scaling replication, the optimal learning rate held across SwiGLU and squared ReLU, batch sizes and new initialisations (zero queries). It broke with learnable RMSNorm gains, sign-based optimizers like Lion, and strong weight decay. Overall muP looks easier to tune than standard parametrisation." },
    ],
    worked: "Fitting a data-model trade-off cheaply with WSD: warm up, then hold the learning rate constant. To evaluate a data size D, take the stable-phase checkpoint at D tokens and run a short decay (about 10% of the run) — a finished model at D for a fraction of a fresh run's cost. Repeat for several D and model sizes, then fit the lower envelope (method 1) or a joint law (method 3).",
    watch: "Early-stopping one long cosine run and treating its intermediate losses as finished runs at smaller data sizes: they were never annealed, so the fit — and the predicted optimum — comes out wrong.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Why do MiniCPM and DeepSeek use a warmup-stable-decay (WSD) learning-rate schedule for their Chinchilla-style analyses?", opts: ["Stable-phase checkpoints can be decayed, so no data size needs a fresh run", "WSD reaches a lower final loss than cosine for every model size tried", "It removes warmup, which is what made the Kaplan fits come out wrong", "The rate stays constant throughout, so no scaling analysis is needed"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 11 - Scaling details.pdf, slides 17-19 and 30",
        expl: "Chinchilla showed a scaling law needs runs trained to completion at each data size — early-stopping a cosine run is not the same — so the cost grows from n to n². WSD holds the rate flat after warmup, so a stable-phase checkpoint can be decayed (about the last 10%) into a finished run at many data sizes for little extra compute." },
      { q: "From what two conditions is muP derived, as width grows?", opts: ["The loss stays Θ(1), and the learning rate is fixed at its small-model value", "Activations stay Θ(1) at initialisation and change by Θ(1) after one step", "Every weight stays Θ(1), and gradients shrink as one over the width", "Each layer's parameter count stays Θ(1), whatever the width is"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 11 - Scaling details.pdf, slides 37-42",
        expl: "A1: activations at initialisation stay order 1 per coordinate; A2: after one gradient step their change is also order 1. Working these through a deep linear network gives width-dependent initialisation scales and per-layer learning rates — for Adam, scaling as 1/fan-in — so the best rate found on a narrow model transfers to a wide one." },
      { q: "How does the DeepSeek recipe differ from Cerebras-GPT's and MiniCPM's?", opts: ["It skips muP and fits the optimal batch size and LR directly", "It uses muP but trains one model size, extrapolating by hand", "It skips scaling laws and copies Chinchilla's 20 per parameter", "It tunes each hyperparameter on the full-size final model"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 11 - Scaling details.pdf, slides 26-32 and 36",
        expl: "Cerebras-GPT and MiniCPM use muP to make hyperparameters scale-invariant. DeepSeek assumes most Transformer hyperparameters already are, collects near-optimal small runs (within 0.25% of the best loss) to fit how batch size and learning rate should scale, sizes the model by IsoFLOP analysis, and uses a WSD-style schedule to keep that cheap." },
      { q: "Which deviation from its assumptions does the lecture find breaks muP's learning-rate transfer?", opts: ["SwiGLU or squared-ReLU activations in place of plain ReLU", "Learnable RMSNorm gains (and, notably, strong weight decay)", "Training with larger or smaller batch sizes than it derives", "Initialising the query matrix at zero, for uniform attention"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 11 - Scaling details.pdf, slides 46-54",
        expl: "In the replication muP held across SwiGLU and squared ReLU, different batch sizes, and new initialisations such as zero queries. It broke with learnable RMSNorm gains (removable at little cost), with sign-based optimizers like Lion, and with strong (0.1) weight decay — perhaps the only significant failure. Still, muP looks easier to tune than standard parametrisation." },
      { q: "Under muP with Adam, a hidden layer's learning rate scales as 1/fan-in. A rate of 0.01 is tuned at fan-in 1024. What rate does that give at fan-in 4096?", num: 0.0025, src: "2025 Lecture 11 - Scaling details.pdf, slides 41-42",
        expl: "0.01 × 1024 / 4096 = 0.0025. Standard parametrisation would keep 0.01 and drift away from the optimum as width grows; muP's rescaling is what lets the optimum found on a narrow proxy carry over." },
      { q: "To find a model's best data size you train it on 1, 2, …, 8 billion tokens. With a cosine schedule each size is a separate run from scratch, 36B tokens in all. With WSD you keep one stable-learning-rate run and branch a decay for each size, the decay being the last 10% of that size's tokens. How many billion tokens of training does WSD need in total? One decimal.", num: 10.8, tol: 0.05, src: "2025 Lecture 11 - Scaling details.pdf, slides 17-19",
        expl: "The stable run only has to reach 90% of the largest size, 7.2B tokens, passing the branch points 0.9, 1.8, …, 7.2B on the way. The decays add 10% of each size: 0.1 × (1 + 2 + … + 8) = 3.6B. That is 10.8B against 36B — the lecture's point that retraining from scratch for every data size turns the cost of a scaling-law fit from n to n², and that restarting from the end of the stable phase avoids it." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.11": {
    takeaway: "There is no one true evaluation: what you measure depends on the question — a purchase decision, raw capability, harms, or feedback for developers. Every benchmark answers four questions (inputs, how the model is called, how outputs are scored, how results are read), and each answer can quietly change what a number means.",
    beats: [
      { t: "What you see, and what it means", d: "Model releases quote similar but not identical benchmarks; leaderboards add cost, usage (OpenRouter) and human preference (Chatbot Arena); and there are vibes. Evaluation looks mechanical — prompts in, average out — but it decides where language models go next." },
      { t: "A framework", d: "Inputs: which use cases, how many hard tail cases, adapted to the model? Calling: which prompt, chain-of-thought, tools or RAG, model or whole agent? Outputs: are references error-free, which metric (pass@k), what cost, which errors are worse? Interpretation: is 91% deployable, was the test seen in training, are we judging a model or a method?" },
      { t: "Perplexity", d: "(1/p(D))^(1/|D|) measures how much probability a model gives a dataset. Since GPT-2 and GPT-3 the field moved to downstream accuracy, but perplexity is smoother for scaling laws and universal. A leaderboard must trust a model's probabilities to sum to one. The maximalist view: drive perplexity to the true entropy and every task is solved — perhaps inefficiently." },
      { t: "Knowledge and instruction following", d: "MMLU: 57 subjects, multiple choice, really testing knowledge. MMLU-Pro: cleaned, 10 choices, chain of thought, 16-33% lower scores. GPQA: PhD experts 65%, Googling non-experts 34%. Humanity's Last Exam: 2,500 filtered questions. For open-ended answers: Chatbot Arena's pairwise human votes, IFEval's checkable constraints, AlpacaEval and WildBench with model judges." },
      { t: "Agents, reasoning, safety", d: "SWE-Bench (2,294 GitHub issues judged by unit tests), CyBench (40 capture-the-flag tasks), MLE-Bench (75 Kaggle competitions); ARC-AGI to isolate reasoning from knowledge. Safety: HarmBench, AIR-Bench's 314 risk categories, jailbreaks such as GCG that transfer to closed models, and pre-deployment testing by safety institutes." },
      { t: "Realism and validity", d: "Most benchmarks are quizzing — the asker knows the answer — while real use is asking. Clio analyses real usage; MedHELM uses clinicians' tasks. Validity needs train-test overlap checked (by exchangeability tests, or by reporting norms) and benchmarks fixed (SWE-Bench Verified, Platinum versions)." },
      { t: "Methods or systems?", d: "Before foundation models we evaluated methods on fixed splits; today we evaluate whole models and systems where anything goes. Exceptions such as the nanoGPT speedrun and DataComp-LM fix the rules to reward algorithmic innovation. Either way, state the rules of the game; HELM's leaderboards let you inspect the individual predictions behind each score." },
    ],
    worked: "Perplexity by hand: a model gives three held-out tokens probabilities 0.5, 0.25 and 0.125. p(D) = 1/64, |D| = 3, so perplexity = 64^(1/3) = 4 — as uncertain as a uniform choice among 4 tokens. In bits, the average loss is (1 + 2 + 3)/3 = 2 bits per token, and 2² = 4.",
    watch: "Comparing two models' scores on 'the same' benchmark when the prompts, shots, chain-of-thought or scoring differ — and treating a score as evidence of capability without checking whether the test set was in the training data.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Why does the lecture warn leaderboard runners about evaluating with perplexity?", opts: ["Perplexity can't be computed for any model whose vocabulary passes 50K", "Perplexity rewards long answers, so verbose models end up ranked too high", "The evaluator has to trust the model's own probabilities to sum to one", "Perplexity needs human raters, which makes it slow and very expensive"], a: 2, src: "lecture_12.py, perplexity",
        expl: "Task accuracy can be computed from a black-box model's outputs alone. Perplexity needs the model to report probabilities, and the evaluator must trust that they are honest and sum to 1 — worse in the days of UNK tokens. Perplexity stays useful: smoother than task accuracy for fitting scaling laws, and universal." },
      { q: "How does Chatbot Arena evaluate models?", opts: ["Experts grade every model's answers to one fixed set of 805 instructions", "GPT-4 judges each response against a checklist written for its prompt", "Unit tests check whether each model's patch resolves a GitHub issue", "Users prompt two anonymous models, pick the better; Elo ranks them"], a: 3, src: "lecture_12.py, instruction_following_benchmarks",
        expl: "A random person types a prompt, sees responses from two anonymised models and says which is better; Elo-style scores come from the pairwise comparisons. The inputs are live, not static, and new models can join. AlpacaEval (805 instructions, GPT-4 judge) and WildBench (checklists) automate this; WildBench correlates 0.95 with the Arena." },
      { q: "Why does the lecture call 'asking' prompts more realistic than 'quizzing' prompts?", opts: ["Asking prompts are shorter, matching the length of real-world traffic", "Quizzing prompts are always multiple choice, unlike what people type", "When asking, the user doesn't know the answer and gets value from it", "Asking prompts come from experts, so their reference answers are right"], a: 2, src: "lecture_12.py, realism",
        expl: "Quizzing is what standardised exams do: the user knows the answer and tests the system. Asking is how systems are actually used — the user doesn't know and wants the answer — so it produces value. Most benchmarks, MMLU included, are far from real use; Clio and MedHELM try to close the gap, though realism and privacy can conflict." },
      { q: "For an open-weight model, which matters more for safety in the lecture's view, capability or propensity — and why?", opts: ["Propensity, since refusal training holds whatever users do afterwards", "Neither, since safety benchmarks only ever measure refusal rates", "Propensity, since anyone can read the weights and audit them freely", "Capability, since anyone with the weights can fine-tune safety away"], a: 3, src: "lecture_12.py, safety_benchmarks",
        expl: "A system can be capable of something yet refuse to do it. For an API model that propensity is what counts; with open weights, refusals can be fine-tuned away, so what the model can do is what matters. Hence the dual-use question about evaluations like CyBench: safety, or capability?" },
      { q: "The lecture defines perplexity as (1/p(D))^(1/|D|). A model gives three test tokens probabilities 0.5, 0.25 and 0.125. What is its perplexity?", num: 4, src: "lecture_12.py, perplexity",
        expl: "p(D) = 0.5 × 0.25 × 0.125 = 1/64, so the perplexity is 64^(1/3) = 4 — as uncertain as choosing uniformly among 4 tokens at each step. Equivalently the average loss is 2 bits per token, and 2² = 4." },
      { q: "The lecture defines perplexity as (1/p(D))^(1/|D|), and pre-training minimises the average per-token cross-entropy. A model's average test loss is 2.0 nats per token. What is its perplexity? Two decimals.", num: 7.39, tol: 0.01, src: "lecture_12.py, perplexity",
        expl: "(1/p(D))^(1/|D|) = exp(−(1/|D|) · ln p(D)) = exp(average loss) = e² ≈ 7.39 — as uncertain as choosing uniformly among about 7.4 tokens at each step. Minimising the training loss is minimising training perplexity, which is why the lecture says pre-training minimises perplexity on the training set." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.12": {
    takeaway: "Data is arguably the most important thing to get right, and the part developers disclose least. Pretraining corpora are built, not found: crawl or collect, convert HTML to text, filter for language and quality (by rules or by classifiers), deduplicate — every step a heuristic with consequences — and all of it under copyright law, licences and terms of service.",
    beats: [
      { t: "Why data, and why secret", d: "Open-weight releases such as Llama 3 describe architecture and training in detail but say almost nothing about data — for competitive reasons and because of copyright liability. Data work is a long tail that scales with human effort. Training moves from lots of lower-quality data (pretraining) to less, better data (mid-training, then post-training)." },
      { t: "Early corpora", d: "BERT: BooksCorpus (7K free self-published books, since taken down) and Wikipedia — documents, not sentences. GPT-2's WebText: 8M pages linked from Reddit posts with at least 3 karma, 40 GB; OpenWebText replicated it. Even Wikipedia can be poisoned with edits timed just before a dump." },
      { t: "Common Crawl and its filters", d: "A monthly crawl since 2008, as raw WARC or lossy WET text; how HTML becomes text measurably changes downstream accuracy. CCNet deduplicates, identifies language and keeps Wikipedia-like text by a 5-gram model. C4 applies manual rules — punctuation-ended lines of five-plus words, no bad words, no code braces — to reach 806 GB." },
      { t: "From GPT-3 to the Pile", d: "GPT-3 used a classifier to find WebText-, Wikipedia- and book-like Common Crawl pages, plus fuzzy deduplication. The Pile's volunteers curated 22 domains (825 GB): Pile-CC, PubMed Central, arXiv, Enron emails, Project Gutenberg, Books3 (from a shadow library, taken down), StackExchange and GitHub. The Stack later cloned 137M repositories for 3.1 TB of permissively licensed code." },
      { t: "The modern web datasets", d: "Gopher's MassiveWeb used manual rules and SafeSearch; LLaMA mixed CCNet, C4, GitHub, Wikipedia, books, arXiv and StackExchange (1.2T tokens). RefinedWeb argued web data is all you need; FineWeb reached 15T tokens; Dolma 3T. DCLM trained a fastText quality classifier and beat other filters; Nemotron-CC argued such filters drop 90% of the data and added rephrasing and ensembles." },
      { t: "Copyright, licences, fair use", d: "Copyright protects original expression fixed in any medium, needs no registration, and covers most of the internet. Using a work needs a licence ('a promise not to sue') or fair use, weighed on purpose, nature, amount and market effect. Training is arguably transformative, but copying is the first step, and models can still hurt creators' markets. Terms of service can add restrictions on top." },
      { t: "Mid- and post-training data", d: "Long context is added late (LongLoRA took Llama 2 7B from 4K to 100K tokens on books and math). Task data turns existing NLP datasets into prompts (Super-Natural Instructions, Flan). Chat data is increasingly synthetic — Alpaca, Vicuna, Baize, WizardLM, OpenHermes — though Llama 2 found 27,540 vendor-annotated examples beat millions of open ones." },
    ],
    worked: "Building a quality filter the DCLM way: collect positives that look like what you want the model to produce (instruction-style answers such as OpenHermes and ELI5) and negatives from ordinary web text (RefinedWeb), 200K each; train a fast linear classifier (fastText); score the whole pool and keep the top fraction. Then check what was thrown away — Nemotron-CC's critique is that such filters discard most of the data.",
    watch: "Treating 'data quality' as an objective property. Every filter encodes a choice — Wikipedia-likeness, Reddit karma, a classifier's idea of good text — and each choice decides what the model will and won't learn.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Why, according to the lecture, do open-weight model developers say so little about their training data?", opts: ["Competitive dynamics, and the risk of copyright liability", "Data volumes are too large to describe in a technical report", "Licences on their datasets forbid naming the sources used", "The data is generated on the fly and never stored anywhere"], a: 0, src: "lecture_13.py, introduction",
        expl: "Llama 3's report, for instance, is transparent about architecture and even training procedure but says almost nothing about data. The lecture's hot take is that data is the most important thing to get right — a long-tail problem that scales with human effort — which is exactly why it is guarded." },
      { q: "Which of these is one of C4's manual filtering heuristics?", opts: ["Keep only the pages a Wikipedia-trained 5-gram model scores as fluent", "Keep lines that end in punctuation and contain at least five words", "Keep pages linked from Reddit posts with at least three karma", "Keep documents a fastText classifier rates as educational"], a: 1, src: "lecture_13.py, t5_c4",
        expl: "C4 started from one April 2019 Common Crawl snapshot (1.4T tokens) and kept lines ending in punctuation with at least 5 words, dropped pages with fewer than 3 sentences, any 'bad word', a '{', 'lorem ipsum' and so on, and kept English (langdetect at 0.99): 806 GB. The others are CCNet's filter, WebText's Reddit rule and classifier filtering." },
      { q: "How did DataComp-LM build its quality classifier?", opts: ["fastText on 200K OpenHermes and ELI5 positives against 200K RefinedWeb", "Human annotators rated a sample of Common Crawl pages from 1 to 5", "GPT-4 labelled every document in the 240T-token pool as keep or drop", "Pages linked from Wikipedia were positives and random pages negatives"], a: 0, src: "lecture_13.py, dclm",
        expl: "Positives: mostly GPT-4-generated instruction data (OpenHermes-2.5) and r/explainlikeimfive answers; negatives: RefinedWeb. A fastText classifier trained on them was run over all of DCLM-pool to give DCLM-baseline (3.8T tokens), beating other filtering methods. Nemotron-CC later argued such filters remove too much — 90% of the data." },
      { q: "Why can a model developer be barred from using a Creative Commons YouTube video, licence or not?", opts: ["Creative Commons licences never cover the training of models", "Terms of service can add restrictions, such as no downloading", "Videos are not 'fixed' works, so copyright does not apply", "Fair use covers only text, so video needs explicit permission"], a: 1, src: "lecture_13.py, copyright",
        expl: "Licences and fair use decide whether copying a copyrighted work is allowed; a platform's terms of service can still restrict how you obtain it — YouTube's prohibit downloading videos, even Creative Commons ones. Most of the internet is copyrighted, and the threshold for copyright is extremely low." },
      { q: "C4 came to 806 GB of text and 156 billion tokens. About how many bytes of text per token is that?", num: 5.17, tol: 0.05, src: "lecture_13.py, t5_c4",
        expl: "806e9 / 156e9 ≈ 5.17 bytes per token — roughly a short English word with its leading space. It is a useful ratio for converting between the gigabytes datasets are reported in and the tokens models are trained on." },
      { q: "C4 filters a page line by line. Of these five lines, how many survive its line rule? 'Home | About | Contact' — 'Click here to subscribe.' — 'The river floods every spring after the snow melts.' — 'Rooms start at 80 euros per night!' — 'Read the full story'", num: 2, src: "lecture_13.py, t5_c4",
        expl: "C4 keeps lines that end in punctuation and have at least 5 words. The navigation bar and 'Read the full story' have no final punctuation; 'Click here to subscribe.' has only 4 words; the river sentence (9 words) and the room-price sentence (7 words, ending in '!') pass. A rule this blunt also drops legitimate short lines — every filter encodes a choice about what counts as text." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.13": {
    takeaway: "Filtering and deduplication are the mechanics under every dataset from the last lecture. Filtering is one framework — given a little target data T and a lot of raw data R, score R and keep what resembles T — with three instantiations (n-gram model, classifier, importance resampling) used for language, quality and toxicity alike. Deduplication at scale is hashing: exact hashes, Bloom filters, and MinHash with locality-sensitive hashing for near duplicates.",
    beats: [
      { t: "Three ways to score text", d: "KenLM: a Kneser-Ney n-gram model, just counting and normalising; CCNet sorts paragraphs by perplexity and keeps the top third. fastText: a bag of word embeddings with H·(V + K) parameters instead of V·K, with the hashing trick for bigrams; for good-versus-bad it is just a linear classifier. DSIR: fit hashed n-gram models to target and raw data and resample by their ratio." },
      { t: "One framework", d: "Estimate a model from R and T, derive a score, keep examples by it. KenLM scores p_T(x), fastText p(T | x) — both keep above a threshold, stochastically — and DSIR scores p_T(x) / p_R(x) and resamples in proportion. Importance resampling is the principled version: sample from q, weight each sample by p/q, resample to get draws from p." },
      { t: "Language identification", d: "fastText's off-the-shelf identifier covers 176 languages; Dolma keeps pages with p(English) ≥ 0.5. It struggles with short text, low-resource languages, dialects, close pairs such as Malay and Indonesian, and code-switching. OpenMathText combined rules, a KenLM perplexity cut and a fastText classifier to curate 14.7B tokens of mathematical text." },
      { t: "Quality and toxicity", d: "GPT-3 trained a linear classifier with Wikipedia, WebText2 and books as positives and Common Crawl as negatives, and kept pages stochastically by score; LLaMA used pages referenced by Wikipedia. phi-1 had GPT-4 label 100K Python files for educational value and trained a random forest on them. Dolma trained fastText toxicity classifiers on the Jigsaw comments." },
      { t: "Exact deduplication and Bloom filters", d: "Duplicates are exact (mirrors, forks) or near (licences, templates, formatting); removing them trains faster and memorises less. Exact dedup hashes each item and keeps one per hash — C4 does it on 3-sentence spans. A Bloom filter tests membership in little memory: a 'no' is certain, a 'yes' is probably right, and more hash functions drive false positives down exponentially." },
      { t: "MinHash and LSH", d: "Near duplicates are pairs with Jaccard similarity above a threshold. MinHash is a hash whose collision probability equals the Jaccard similarity. LSH sharpens that into a threshold: split n = b·r hash functions into b bands of r, and call A and B a match if every hash in some band agrees, giving 1 − (1 − s^r)^b. More rows move the threshold up; more bands move it down." },
    ],
    worked: "LSH at s = 0.8 with b = 5 bands of r = 10. One band matches only if all ten MinHashes agree: 0.8^10 ≈ 0.107. A band fails with probability 0.893, and all five fail with probability 0.893^5 ≈ 0.567, so the pair is flagged with probability ≈ 0.433. With the paper's setting n = 9000, b = 20, r = 450 the phase transition sits at (1/20)^(1/450) ≈ 0.993.",
    watch: "Reading a Bloom filter's 'yes' as proof. It can be wrong — only a 'no' is certain — so a deduplication pass built on one will occasionally drop an item that was never seen before, at a rate fixed by the memory and the number of hash functions.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "In the lecture's general filtering framework, how does importance resampling (DSIR) use its score?", opts: ["It keeps x when p_T(x) clears a threshold, stochastically", "It keeps x when a classifier's p(T | x) clears a threshold", "It resamples x in proportion to the ratio p_T(x) / p_R(x)", "It keeps x when its Jaccard similarity to T clears a cut"], a: 2, src: "lecture_14.py, filtering_summary",
        expl: "The three instantiations differ in the score: a generative model of T (KenLM) uses p_T(x), a discriminative classifier (fastText) uses p(T | x), and DSIR uses the importance weight p_T(x) / p_R(x), resampling rather than thresholding. Jaccard similarity belongs to deduplication, not filtering." },
      { q: "phi-1 trained a 1.3B model on a classifier-filtered subset of The Stack's Python. How did it compare with training on the unfiltered Python subset?", opts: ["Lower HumanEval, though it trained in a third of the steps", "Equal HumanEval, from a model a tenth the size", "Equal HumanEval, with far less memorised code", "Higher HumanEval, and after fewer training steps"], a: 3, src: "lecture_14.py, quality_filtering",
        expl: "12.19% on HumanEval after 96K steps on the raw Python subset, against 17.68% after 36K steps on the filtered one. The positives came from GPT-4 judging 100K files for 'educational value'; a random forest on codegen embeddings then selected the rest." },
      { q: "A Bloom filter answers 'no' when asked whether an item is in the set. What does that tell you?", opts: ["The item is probably absent, with a small false-negative rate", "The item was inserted but has since been deleted from the set", "The item is definitely not in the set", "Nothing until the remaining hash functions have been checked"], a: 2, src: "lecture_14.py, bloom_filter",
        expl: "Every inserted item sets all of its k bits, so an item that was inserted can never find one of its bits empty: 'no' is certain. 'Yes' can be a false positive when other items happened to set all k bits. Bloom filters also cannot delete — clearing a bit could erase other items." },
      { q: "C4 deduplicates by exact match on 3-sentence spans, keeping one copy of each. What drawback does the lecture warn about?", opts: ["Hashing every span needs memory quadratic in the size of the corpus", "Exact span matching cannot catch any duplicated whole documents", "Documents shorter than three sentences are all silently discarded", "Cutting a span from mid-document can leave the remainder incoherent"], a: 3, src: "lecture_14.py, exact_deduplication",
        expl: "Removing all but one copy of a repeated span deletes it from the middle of other documents, which may then not read coherently. Exact matching is simple, precise and parallelises as MapReduce, but it misses near duplicates — the job of MinHash and LSH." },
      { q: "With LSH, two documents with Jaccard similarity 0.8 are hashed with b = 5 bands of r = 10 MinHash functions each. What is the probability that they collide? Three decimals.", num: 0.433, tol: 0.001, src: "lecture_14.py, locality_sensitive_hashing",
        expl: "A band matches only if all r hashes agree: 0.8^10 ≈ 0.1074. They collide if some band matches: 1 − (1 − 0.1074)^5 ≈ 1 − 0.5667 = 0.433. Raising r makes matching harder; raising b makes it easier." },
      { q: "fastText's bag of word embeddings uses vocabulary V = 8192, hidden size H = 16 and K = 64 classes. Counting the embedding and the output head weights (no biases), how many parameters is that? (digits only, no commas)", num: 132096, src: "lecture_14.py, fasttext_main",
        expl: "An 8192 × 16 embedding (131,072) plus a 16 × 64 head (1,024) is H·(V + K) = 132,096 — a quarter of the 8192 × 64 = 524,288 that a direct bag-of-words classifier would need." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.14": {
    takeaway: "Pretraining gets you GPT-3; post-training gets you InstructGPT. The standard recipe is imitation, then optimisation: supervised fine-tuning on demonstrations extracts behaviours the base model already has, and RLHF then maximises a reward learned from pairwise preferences — first with PPO, now mostly with DPO. Both stages are only as good as their data, and both have side effects worth watching.",
    beats: [
      { t: "What instruction data looks like", d: "FLAN recasts existing NLP tasks as prompts (subject lines, topic labels, highlights); Alpaca is short model-written answers; OpenAssistant is long human-written answers, some with references. They differ in length, bullet points and knowledge — and length and style strongly sway preference evaluations, human and GPT-based alike, while mattering little on other benchmarks." },
      { t: "What SFT can and cannot teach", d: "Folklore, backed by Schulman and Gekhman: fine-tuning on facts the model does not know teaches it to hallucinate. A citation in a demonstration may teach the habit of citing more than the source. SFT works best when it extracts pretraining behaviours rather than adding new ones; even factually correct data can hurt." },
      { t: "Safety and scaling SFT", d: "A little instruction tuning changes safety a lot: about 500 safety examples mixed into Alpaca-style data make models follow safety guidelines. The hard part is balancing that against over-refusal. To scale instruction tuning, 'midtraining' mixes instruction data into the end of pretraining, then runs a short tuning round — without catastrophic forgetting." },
      { t: "Why optimise", d: "Imitation fits p̂(y | x) to a reference distribution and needs samples from it; optimisation finds the policy that maximises a reward we can measure — the LM is a policy, not a model of a distribution. Scalar or pairwise feedback is far cheaper than demonstrations, and people do not always write the output they would prefer (the generator–validator gap)." },
      { t: "Preference data", d: "Pairwise comparisons from crowdworkers (InstructGPT used about 40, via Scale and Upwork) are hard to make high-quality and verified; annotators may lean on GPT-4; their demographics and style preferences shift the result. GPT-4 judgments agree with humans about as well as humans agree with each other, so AI feedback (UltraFeedback, Constitutional AI) is common." },
      { t: "PPO, DPO and side effects", d: "PPO: policy gradients with variance controls — linearise (TRPO), then clip the ratio — and finicky in practice. DPO assumes a nonparametric policy, solves for the implied reward, and fits it with the pairwise loss: a positive gradient on the chosen answer, a negative one on the rejected, weighted by the implied reward's error. Watch for longer outputs, reward overoptimisation and lost calibration." },
    ],
    worked: "Checking one SFT example by hand. An Alpaca response defines avg_list(nums) — sum the list, divide by its length — and claims print(avg_list([4, 3, 6, 10, 8])) outputs 6.4. The sum is 31, the length 5, so the function returns 6.2. Fine-tuning on that pair teaches the model to state a wrong result with confidence; this is why crowdsourced and generated data need someone to check correctness.",
    watch: "Reading a rising reward as rising quality. Past a point, optimising a learned reward overfits it — the lecture's curves show it for human and noisy-LM preferences — and the gains can be length and style rather than substance.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "How does the optimisation view of post-training (RLHF) differ from imitation (SFT), as the lecture frames it?", opts: ["It fits the model's p(y | x) to a reference distribution using expert samples", "It maximises a reward that it can measure, and treats the LM as a policy", "It trains on far more data, scraped from the web rather than written", "It updates only the final layers, while SFT updates every parameter"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 15 - RLHF Alignment.pdf, slide 31",
        expl: "Imitation is pure generative modelling: fit p̂(y | x) ≈ p*(y | x) from samples of a reference policy. Optimisation finds p̂ maximising E[R(y, x)] for a measurable reward — so the LM is a policy, not a model of some distribution. The first option describes SFT." },
      { q: "What does the folklore the lecture cites say happens when you fine-tune a model on facts it does not already know?", opts: ["It learns to hallucinate, answering without basis", "It memorises the facts but cannot use them elsewhere", "It forgets as many facts from pretraining", "It learns them reliably if each appears once"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 15 - RLHF Alignment.pdf, slides 20-21",
        expl: "Schulman (2023) and Gekhman et al. (2023): training on unknown facts teaches the model to produce confident answers it has no basis for. Hence the takeaways — you may not want to fine-tune on tail knowledge even when that is the use case, and correctness feedback from RL could help." },
      { q: "Safety-tuning with even a little data changes a model's safety profile a lot. What does the lecture call the real challenge?", opts: ["Collecting enough harmful prompts to cover every category", "Balancing safety against over-refusals of benign requests", "Keeping the safety data out of the pretraining corpus", "Stopping safety gains from vanishing after a few epochs"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 15 - RLHF Alignment.pdf, slides 23-25",
        expl: "About 500 safety examples added to Alpaca-style data significantly improve safety. The hard part is that the same tuning makes models refuse things they should answer, so the two have to be balanced." },
      { q: "Which describes DPO's update, as the lecture summarises it?", opts: ["Up-weight the chosen answer, down-weight the rejected, scaled by error", "Fresh on-policy rollouts scored by a separately trained reward model", "Supervised training on the chosen answers only, discarding the rejected", "Sampling 1024 outputs per prompt and fine-tuning on the best of them"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 15 - RLHF Alignment.pdf, slides 54-58",
        expl: "DPO drops the reward model and the on-policy machinery. It solves the KL-regularised RLHF objective for the 'implied reward' under a nonparametric assumption, then fits that reward with the pairwise loss — 'pos gradient on good, neg gradient on bad', scaled by the implied reward model's prediction error. The other options are the PPO setup and the simpler alternatives people tried first." },
      { q: "The overoptimisation results compare RLHF on human, noisy-LM and noiseless-LM preferences. Where did optimising the reward NOT overfit past a point?", opts: ["Human preferences", "Noisy LM preferences", "Noiseless LM preferences", "None — every setting overfit"], a: 2, src: "2025 Lecture 15 - RLHF Alignment.pdf, slide 64",
        expl: "Across many RLHF-style optimisers, true quality first rises and then falls as the learned reward keeps climbing — for human and noisy simulated preferences. With noiseless LM preferences there is nothing for the policy to exploit, which points at label noise as the driver." },
      { q: "One Alpaca example on the slides defines avg_list(nums), which sums a list and divides by its length, and claims print(avg_list([4, 3, 6, 10, 8])) outputs 6.4. What does it actually print?", num: 6.2, tol: 0.001, src: "2025 Lecture 15 - RLHF Alignment.pdf, slide 11",
        expl: "4 + 3 + 6 + 10 + 8 = 31 and 31 / 5 = 6.2. The example's stated output is wrong — a reminder of the lecture's point that getting annotators (or models) to really check correctness is hard, and that SFT on a wrong answer teaches it." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.15": {
    takeaway: "RLHF cannot be scaled cleanly because a learned reward gets overoptimised. Reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards sidesteps that by working where the reward is exactly what we want — is the maths answer right, do the tests pass. GRPO made it simple: PPO without the value model, with the advantage taken as a z-score within a group of rollouts. DeepSeek R1, Kimi K1.5 and Qwen 3 show the recipes, and their flaws.",
    beats: [
      { t: "PPO, in practice", d: "Policy gradients have too much variance; TRPO linearises around the current policy; PPO clips the probability ratio (AlpacaFarm uses a clip range of 0.2). For language models the reward is shaped: a per-token KL penalty against the reference model plus the full reward on the last token, with advantages from generalised advantage estimation. It works, but it is complicated and the value model is memory-hungry." },
      { t: "Why not PPO, why not DPO", d: "PPO needs a value model and a complicated implementation. DPO needs pairwise, Bradley-Terry-style data, and it is offline — while a verifier gives each rollout its own score. GRPO starts from PPO, removes the value function, and sets each rollout's advantage to its reward's z-score within the group sampled for the same prompt." },
      { t: "GRPO's flaws", d: "Subtracting any state-dependent baseline keeps the policy gradient unbiased; dividing by the group's standard deviation does not, and it upweights questions that are too easy or too hard. Normalising by response length biases it further. Dr. GRPO (Liu et al. 2025) removes both, which brings it close to REINFORCE with a leave-one-out baseline." },
      { t: "DeepSeek R1", d: "R1-Zero: GRPO on DeepSeek-V3 with only accuracy and format rewards — no process reward models, no MCTS, both listed among the unsuccessful attempts. Its chains of thought grew and an 'aha' moment appeared, though follow-ups argue the length comes from the biased objective and the base model already says 'aha'. R1 adds a long-CoT SFT start, a language-consistency reward and a final SFT/RLHF stage, then distils 800K traces into Qwen 2.5." },
      { t: "Kimi K1.5", d: "Data curation drops multiple-choice and true/false questions (guesses earn false positives) and keeps only problems the model fails at best-of-8. Its RL loss comes from a DPO-style derivation with a squared-loss surrogate. A length reward pushes correct answers to be short; a curriculum samples problems in proportion to 1 − success rate. RL infrastructure matters: rollouts are slow inference, often in another framework, with uneven batch lengths." },
      { t: "Qwen 3", d: "The same playbook — filter by best-of-n difficulty, drop what the model gets right without a chain of thought, drop near-duplicates of validation data, check CoT quality by hand — then GRPO on only 3,995 examples. Thinking-mode fusion mixes thinking and non-thinking data under tags. General-purpose RLHF afterwards costs a little maths and STEM ability." },
    ],
    worked: "GRPO's advantage for one prompt. Sample four rollouts; the verifier scores them 1, 0, 0, 0. The group mean is 0.25 and the standard deviation (dividing by n) is √(0.25 × 0.75) ≈ 0.433. The correct rollout's advantage is (1 − 0.25) / 0.433 ≈ 1.73; each wrong one gets (0 − 0.25) / 0.433 ≈ −0.58. Had all four been right, every advantage would be 0 — and the division by a near-zero standard deviation is why a small stability term (1e-4) is added.",
    watch: "Treating GRPO's standard-deviation division as harmless normalisation. It is not a valid baseline: it biases the gradient and re-weights questions by how easy or hard they are, and length normalisation adds its own bias — part of why R1-Zero's responses grew.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Why does the lecture say DPO is a poor fit for RL with verifiable rewards?", opts: ["It needs a separate value model, which is memory-hungry to train", "It cannot use a KL penalty against a reference model at all", "It only works for models smaller than about seven billion parameters", "The data is not inherently pairwise, and DPO itself is offline"], a: 3, src: "2025 Lecture 16 - RLVR.pdf, slide 29",
        expl: "A verifier scores each rollout on its own, not as a Bradley-Terry comparison between two, and DPO trains on a fixed dataset rather than the policy's own samples (it can be made online only by iterating). The value model is the objection to PPO, not DPO." },
      { q: "What is the problem with GRPO dividing each group's rewards by their standard deviation?", opts: ["It makes the advantage depend on response length, so longer answers win", "It is not a valid baseline, so the policy gradient is biased", "It requires a learned value model to estimate the deviation", "It discards every rollout whose reward equals the group mean"], a: 1, src: "2025 Lecture 16 - RLVR.pdf, slides 34-36",
        expl: "Any state-dependent term can be subtracted from the reward without biasing the policy gradient; a division cannot. The standard-deviation term upweights questions that are too easy or too hard. The length bias is a separate term — the normaliser — which Dr. GRPO also removes." },
      { q: "Which rewards did DeepSeek R1-Zero's reinforcement learning use?", opts: ["Step-by-step scores from a learned process reward model", "Human pairwise preferences between complete responses", "Answer accuracy, plus a format reward for thinking tags", "Value estimates from Monte Carlo tree search over steps"], a: 2, src: "2025 Lecture 16 - RLVR.pdf, slides 39-40",
        expl: "R1-Zero applied GRPO to DeepSeek-V3 with accuracy rewards (is it correct?) and format rewards (use thinking tags). It did not use process supervision; PRMs and MCTS appear in the paper's unsuccessful-attempts section, which ended speculation that they were necessary." },
      { q: "Why did Kimi K1.5 exclude multiple-choice and true/false questions from its RL data?", opts: ["A lucky guess can be scored as correct, giving a false positive", "Their answers are much too short to train long chains of thought", "Their verifiers could not parse letter-coded answers reliably", "The base model had already memorised most of those benchmarks"], a: 0, src: "2025 Lecture 16 - RLVR.pdf, slide 53",
        expl: "With a verifiable reward, a lucky guess on a 4-way or 2-way question earns full reward, reinforcing reasoning that did not produce it. Kimi also kept only problems the model fails at best-of-8, so the RL signal comes from questions it cannot already solve." },
      { q: "A GRPO group of four rollouts scores 1, 0, 0, 0. Taking the advantage as the z-score within the group (standard deviation dividing by n, ignoring the small stability term), what is the correct rollout's advantage? Three decimals.", num: 1.732, tol: 0.01, src: "2025 Lecture 16 - RLVR.pdf, slides 30-32",
        expl: "Mean 0.25; variance 0.25 × 0.75 = 0.1875, so σ ≈ 0.433. Advantage (1 − 0.25) / 0.433 = √3 ≈ 1.732. (With the n − 1 standard deviation it would be 1.5 — the convention matters, which is one reason implementations differ.)" },
      { q: "Kimi K1.5's curriculum samples problems in proportion to (1 − success rate). How many times as often is a problem the model solves 40% of the time sampled as one it solves 90% of the time?", num: 6, src: "2025 Lecture 16 - RLVR.pdf, slide 56",
        expl: "(1 − 0.4) / (1 − 0.9) = 0.6 / 0.1 = 6. The aim is to stop spending rollouts on problems that are already solved; together with easy-to-hard difficulty labels it forms the curriculum." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.0.16": {
    takeaway: "Policy gradient, worked through in code. For a language model the state is the prompt plus the response so far, the action is the next token, and the reward comes once at the end. The gradient of expected reward is just SFT weighted by reward — unbiased but noisy — and a baseline cuts the noise without changing the answer. GRPO takes its baseline from the group of responses to the same prompt; a toy sorting task shows how much each choice matters.",
    beats: [
      { t: "RL for language models", d: "State s: prompt plus response so far; action: the next token; transitions are deterministic (s' = s + a), so planning and test-time compute are possible. Rewards are outcome rewards on the whole response, and verifiable — computed deterministically — so discounting and bootstrapping matter less. The policy is just the (fine-tuned) language model; the objective is expected reward." },
      { t: "Naive policy gradient", d: "∇E[R] = E[∇ log π(a | s) R(s, a)]: sample a response, then update as in SFT but weighted by its reward. With rewards in {0, 1} it updates only on correct responses — SFT on a dataset that changes as the policy does. Sparse rewards make it very noisy; RLHF's learned reward models are more continuous." },
      { t: "Baselines and advantages", d: "Maximise E[R − b(s)] instead: it differs from E[R] by a constant that does not depend on the policy, so the gradient stays unbiased while its variance can fall. The optimal b(s) is hard to compute; the usual heuristic is the mean reward E[R | s] = V(s), which makes R − b(s) the advantage A(s, a) = Q(s, a) − V(s)." },
      { t: "GRPO's pieces in code", d: "Generate several responses per prompt, score them, and turn rewards into deltas: raw, centred on the group mean, normalised by the group standard deviation, or max-only. The loss is −log-prob × delta (naive), or a probability ratio against the old policy, optionally clipped (ε = 0.01 here). p_old must be frozen; an optional KL penalty uses the estimator E_p[q/p − log(q/p) − 1]." },
      { t: "What the toy experiments show", d: "On sorting three numbers, raw rewards learn little. Centring helps — below-average responses get pushed down, and a prompt whose responses all score the same gives no update — but training still sticks in local optima. Normalising by the standard deviation adds little here, and Dr. GRPO drops it to avoid length bias. RL systems are harder than pretraining: inference workloads and several models to manage." },
    ],
    worked: "The two-state baseline example. In s1 the actions earn 11 and 9; in s2 they earn 0 and 2. Raw, the update would favour s1's worse action (9) over s2's better one (2). Subtract b(s1) = 10 and b(s2) = 1 and the rewards become +1, −1, −1, +1: each state's better action is pushed up and its worse one down, and the sample standard deviation of the update weights falls from about 5.32 to about 1.15.",
    watch: "Forgetting to freeze p_old. If the old policy's probability is computed with gradients on, the ratio p / p_old is identically 1 and its gradient is exactly zero — the code runs, the loss is finite, and nothing learns. Compute p_old under no_grad and treat it as a constant.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Unlike RL for robotics, transitions for a language model are deterministic: the next state is the old state plus the generated token. What does the lecture say that makes possible?", opts: ["Dropping the reward and training on the transitions alone", "Discounting future rewards using a separately learned value function", "Treating the problem as supervised learning from the start", "Planning and test-time compute over possible continuations"], a: 3, src: "lecture_17.py, rl_setup_for_language_models",
        expl: "T(s' | s, a) is deterministic, s' = s + a, so you can plan and spend test-time compute in a way you cannot with a physical robot; the states are also 'made up', which gives a lot of flexibility. Discounting and bootstrapping are less applicable here, because rewards are outcome rewards on the whole response." },
      { q: "With rewards in {0, 1} (correct or not), what does the naive policy gradient update amount to?", opts: ["SFT on correct responses, on data that shifts with the policy", "SFT on every response, with incorrect ones weighted negatively", "A pairwise loss pushing correct responses above incorrect ones", "A value-function regression on each prompt's success rate"], a: 0, src: "lecture_17.py, policy_gradient",
        expl: "The update is ∇ log π(a | s) R(s, a): with R = 0 the term vanishes, so only correct responses are reinforced — like SFT, but on the policy's own samples, which change as it learns. Pushing incorrect responses down needs a baseline (centred rewards)." },
      { q: "In the sorting experiments, what does centring each prompt's rewards on the group mean change, compared with raw rewards?", opts: ["Every response gets a positive update, scaled by its own reward", "Below-average ones go down; an all-equal group gives no update", "The update becomes independent of how long each response is", "Training reaches the global optimum on all three prompts"], a: 1, src: "lecture_17.py, experiments",
        expl: "Subtracting the mean makes deltas negative for responses below the group average, and zero for all of them when every response scored the same. It helps, but the lecture notes training still gets stuck in local optima — RL is not trivial." },
      { q: "Why must p_old be frozen (computed without gradients) in the GRPO ratio p(a | s) / p_old(a | s)?", opts: ["Otherwise the clipping range collapses to zero width and nothing is clipped", "Otherwise the KL penalty becomes negative and diverges", "Otherwise both models share an optimiser state and drift", "Otherwise the ratio is identically 1 and its gradient is zero"], a: 3, src: "lecture_17.py, freezing_parameters",
        expl: "If p_old is the same function of the same weights with gradients on, p / p_old is the constant 1 and the gradient is 0 — the trace shows grad = 0.0. Under torch.no_grad() p_old is a constant and the gradient of sigmoid(w) / sigmoid(2) at w = 2 is 1 − sigmoid(2) ≈ 0.119." },
      { q: "Two states: in s1 the actions earn 11 and 9, in s2 they earn 0 and 2. With baselines b(s1) = 10 and b(s2) = 1, what is the sample standard deviation (dividing by n − 1) of the four baselined rewards? Three decimals.", num: 1.155, tol: 0.001, src: "lecture_17.py, policy_gradient",
        expl: "The baselined rewards are +1, −1, −1, +1: mean 0, sum of squares 4, so √(4/3) ≈ 1.155. The raw rewards 11, 9, 0, 2 have standard deviation √(85/3) ≈ 5.323 — the baseline cuts the spread by more than four times without changing which action is better in each state." },
      { q: "The lecture's partial-credit reward gives one point for each prompt number that appears in the response and one for each adjacent pair in the response that is in order (x ≤ y). What does it give the response [0, 3, 1, 2] to the prompt [3, 1, 0, 2]?", num: 6, src: "lecture_17.py, simple_task",
        expl: "All four prompt numbers appear (4 points); of the adjacent pairs (0, 3), (3, 1) and (1, 2), two are in order (2 points): 6. The exact-position reward gives the same response only 1 (just the leading 0 is in place) — the partial-credit reward is the denser signal." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.1.0": {
    takeaway: "One call to pipeline() hides three steps — preprocess, run the model, post-process — and choosing a model is mostly choosing which half of the Transformer the task needs: an encoder to understand the whole input, a decoder to generate, or both to turn one sequence into another.",
    beats: [
      { t: "NLP is the field, LLMs are the current tool", d: "NLP covers classifying sentences, labelling each word, extracting answers and generating text. LLMs are a subset defined by scale, by doing many tasks without task-specific training, and by learning from examples in the prompt. Their limits come with them: hallucination, bias, finite context windows and heavy compute." },
      { t: "pipeline() is three steps in one call", d: "The text is preprocessed into something the model can read, the model runs, and its raw predictions are post-processed into a label and a score. The default model for a task is downloaded once and cached; running it again reuses the cached weights, not a cached answer." },
      { t: "The task decides what the output can be", d: "Zero-shot classification takes the candidate labels at call time, so no fine-tuning on your labels is needed. Question answering EXTRACTS a span from the context you pass; it does not generate an answer. Named-entity recognition regroups sub-word pieces such as S, ##yl, ##va, ##in back into one entity." },
      { t: "Pretrain once, fine-tune many times", d: "Language models are pretrained self-supervised — the objective is computed from the text itself, causal (next word) or masked (fill the blank). Fine-tuning then trains on labelled data for one task, needing far less data, time and money than training from scratch, which is why sharing weights matters." },
      { t: "Encoder, decoder, and the mask that keeps training honest", d: "The encoder's attention sees the whole sentence; the decoder generates and may only attend to words already produced. In training the decoder is fed the whole target, so an attention mask hides positions at and after the word being predicted. Architecture is the skeleton; a checkpoint is the weights loaded into it." },
      { t: "Every task is a body plus a head", d: "BERT for classification adds a linear head on the final [CLS] state; for token classification, a head per token; for question answering, start and end logits for a span. GPT-2 predicts the next token with labels shifted by one. ViT cuts an image into 16x16 patches and treats them as tokens, with a [CLS] token just like BERT." },
      { t: "Pick the architecture by asking three questions", d: "Bidirectional or one-way understanding? Generating new text or analysing existing text? Transforming one sequence into another? Classification, NER and extractive QA point to an encoder; open generation and chat to a decoder; translation and summarisation to an encoder-decoder. Full attention is quadratic in length, which is what Longformer's local windows and Reformer's LSH attention work around." },
      { t: "Inference has two phases with different costs", d: "Prefill tokenizes and processes the whole prompt at once, which is computationally intensive; it sets the time to first token. Decode then produces one token at a time, each step attending to everything before it, which is memory-intensive. Temperature, top-k, top-p, repetition penalties and stop sequences shape what decode picks; the KV cache trades memory for not recomputing the past." },
      { t: "Bias survives fine-tuning", d: "bert-base-uncased, trained on Wikipedia and BookCorpus rather than the open web, still fills 'This woman works as a [MASK]' with gendered jobs. Fine-tuning on your own data does not remove what pretraining put there." },
    ],
    worked: "Before reaching for a model, name the task's output. A label for the whole text is a classification head on an encoder; a label per token is token classification; a span copied out of a document is extractive QA; new text is a decoder, or an encoder-decoder when the new text is a transformation of an input. Then find a checkpoint already fine-tuned for that task on the Hub and run it through pipeline() before writing any training code.",
    watch: "Treating the question-answering pipeline as if it could answer from knowledge. It extracts a span from the context it is given, so a context that does not contain the answer still yields a confident-looking span — just a wrong one.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "You call a sentiment-analysis pipeline on a raw sentence and get back a label and a score. What happened in between?", opts: ["The text was preprocessed into model inputs, the model ran, and its predictions were post-processed into a label and score", "The model read the raw string directly; pipeline() only chose which pretrained checkpoint to download for the task", "A pretrained model was briefly fine-tuned on your sentence, and the fine-tuned copy produced the label and score", "The sentence was looked up in a cache of earlier answers, and a stored prediction was returned when one matched"], a: 0, src: "1.3 Transformers, what can they do? — Working with pipelines",
        expl: "pipeline() connects a model with its preprocessing and postprocessing. The model never sees a raw string, and nothing is trained at call time. What gets cached is the downloaded model, so a second run skips the download — not the computation." },
      { q: "The question-answering pipeline is given a question and a context paragraph. What can its answer be?", opts: ["Any sentence the model composes from its training data, guided by the context", "A summary of the context rewritten so that it addresses the question", "Only a span copied out of the context, located by start and end positions", "A yes or no, with the context used only to compute the confidence score"], a: 2, src: "1.3 Question answering; 1.5 Question answering",
        expl: "The pipeline extracts information from the provided context; it does not generate. Under the hood a span-classification head predicts start and end logits over the context's tokens, so the answer is always a piece of the text you passed in." },
      { q: "During training a translation decoder is fed the entire target sentence at once. Why can it not simply copy the word it is asked to predict?", opts: ["Because the target sentence is shuffled before it reaches the decoder", "Because an attention mask hides that position and every later one", "Because the decoder reads only the encoder's output, never the target", "Because training uses a different language for the target each time"], a: 1, src: "1.4 The original architecture",
        expl: "Feeding the whole target speeds training, so the decoder is masked: predicting the fourth word, its attention can use positions 1 to 3 only. The second attention layer still sees the encoder's output for the WHOLE source sentence, because word order differs between languages." },
      { q: "ViT splits a 224 x 224 image into non-overlapping 16 x 16 patches. How many patch embeddings does the encoder receive, before the [CLS] token is added?", num: 196, src: "1.5 Image classification",
        expl: "Each side holds 224 / 16 = 14 patches, so there are 14 x 14 = 196. Each patch becomes one embedding — the image is 'tokenized' into patches — and a learnable [CLS] embedding is prepended, so the sequence is 197 long." },
      { q: "You need to tag every word of a sentence as person, organisation, location or none. Which architecture does the chapter point you to, and why?", opts: ["Decoder-only, because modern LLMs are decoders and handle every task best", "Encoder-decoder, because the output is itself a sequence of labels to generate", "Encoder-only, because each word's label depends on context on both sides of it", "Decoder-only, because labelling one word at a time is a left-to-right process"], a: 2, src: "1.6 Choosing the right architecture",
        expl: "Named-entity recognition is understanding, not generation: a word's label can depend on the words after it as well as before. Encoders attend in both directions, and a token-classification head on top gives one label per token." },
      { q: "Users say the first word of a chatbot's reply takes a long time to appear, but the rest then streams quickly. Which phase is the bottleneck?", opts: ["Decode, because each new token must attend to all previous ones", "Prefill, because the whole prompt is processed at once first", "Sampling, because top-p must sort the vocabulary every step", "Tokenization of the output, which runs after generation ends"], a: 1, src: "1.8 The two-phase inference process; Key performance metrics",
        expl: "Time to first token is primarily set by prefill, which tokenizes and runs the entire input through the model before anything is generated. Decode then governs time per output token; it is the memory-intensive phase, and the KV cache is what speeds it up." },
    ],
  },

  "ai310.1.1": {
    takeaway: "Supervised fine-tuning is next-token cross-entropy on your own examples — so the decisions that matter are which tokens carry the loss, what format the data is in, and whether the end-of-sequence token matches the chat template.",
    beats: [
      { t: "The whole method in one call", d: "SFTTrainer takes a model name and a training dataset and trains. Everything else on the page is a choice about the data, the loss mask or the cost of the run." },
      { t: "Four dataset shapes", d: "Language modelling (a text field) or prompt-completion, each either standard (plain strings) or conversational (role and content messages). A conversational dataset has the chat template applied automatically. Anything else is mapped into one of these first — the medical-reasoning example folds Question, chain of thought and Response into a prompt and a completion." },
      { t: "The loss is the pretraining loss, on chosen tokens", d: "Token-level cross-entropy: minimise $-\\sum_t \\log p_\\theta(y_t \\mid y_{\\lt t})$. Prompt and completion are concatenated and tokenized together; the labels are the inputs shifted by one; padding positions get the ignore index (-100) so they add nothing to the loss." },
      { t: "What the logs mean", d: "loss is averaged over NON-masked tokens; mean_token_accuracy is the share of non-masked tokens whose top-1 prediction is right; entropy is the spread of the predicted distribution; grad_norm is measured before clipping; num_tokens counts what has been processed." },
      { t: "Choosing which tokens teach", d: "With a prompt-completion dataset the loss covers the completion only, by default (completion_only_loss=False turns that off). With a conversational dataset, assistant_only_loss=True restricts it to the assistant's replies — which needs generation and endgeneration markers in the chat template; TRL patches them in for known families such as Qwen3." },
      { t: "Making runs cheaper", d: "packing=True fills each sequence with several examples. model_init_kwargs passes loading options such as bfloat16 through SFTConfig. A LoraConfig as peft_config trains adapters instead of the whole model, usually at a higher learning rate (about 1e-4) because only the new parameters are learned." },
      { t: "Instruction tuning a base model", d: "Needs a chat template — role markers, special tokens, turn boundaries — and a conversational dataset; chat_template_path can borrow one from another model. A base model that already ships a template still needs its EOS token aligned with it (eos_token in SFTConfig), or replies do not terminate correctly." },
      { t: "Tools and images", d: "Tool-calling data carries tool calls, tool-role responses and a tools column of JSON schemas. Vision-language data adds image or images columns; set max_length=None there, because truncation can cut image tokens and break training." },
    ],
    worked: "Before the first run, write down which tokens of one example should carry loss, then check the setting that does it: completion-only for prompt-completion data, assistant_only_loss for conversations, nothing extra for plain text. Then train for a few steps and read mean_token_accuracy and loss — both are over non-masked tokens, so they tell you whether the mask is what you meant.",
    watch: "Fine-tuning a base model into a chat model and leaving the EOS token as it was. The model learns the template's turns but never the token that ends them, so it keeps generating past its answer; the fix is eos_token in SFTConfig, matched to the template.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "What does SFTTrainer minimise for each training example?", opts: ["A reward that a separate preference model assigns to the whole completion", "The squared distance between output and reference embeddings", "The number of positions where the output differs from the reference", "The negative log-likelihood of each target token given those before it"], a: 3, src: "Looking deeper into the SFT method — Computing the loss",
        expl: "SFT is token-level cross-entropy, the same objective as pretraining: $-\\sum_t \\log p_\\theta(y_t \\mid y_{\\lt t})$. The labels are the input shifted by one position, and masked positions (padding, or tokens excluded by the loss settings) contribute nothing. No reward model is involved; that is the next stage of alignment." },
      { q: "You pass a prompt-completion dataset to SFTTrainer and change no settings. Which tokens does the loss see?", opts: ["Only the completion tokens; the prompt tokens are masked out", "Every token, prompt and completion alike, just as in pretraining", "Only the prompt tokens, so the model learns to read inputs", "Only the last token of each completion, just before EOS"], a: 0, src: "Customization — Train on completion only",
        expl: "For prompt-completion data the trainer computes the loss on the completion only by default; completion_only_loss=False puts the prompt back in. The prompt is still in the input — the model conditions on it — it just is not something the model is graded on reproducing." },
      { q: "Default completion-only loss. A logging interval holds one example: 4 prompt tokens, all predicted correctly at top-1, and 6 completion tokens, of which 3 are predicted correctly. What mean_token_accuracy is logged?", num: 0.5, src: "Logged metrics; Train on completion only",
        expl: "mean_token_accuracy is the proportion of NON-masked tokens whose top-1 prediction matches. The prompt is masked, so it is in neither the numerator nor the denominator: 3 / 6 = 0.5. Counting it would give 7 / 10 = 0.7 and flatter the model with tokens it was never trained to produce." },
      { q: "assistant_only_loss=True needs the chat template to contain generation and endgeneration markers. What job are those markers doing?", opts: ["Telling the tokenizer where each turn ends so that turns can be packed", "Switching the model into generation mode, which training turns off", "Placing the EOS token after every user message in the conversation", "Marking which rendered tokens are the reply, the only ones given loss"], a: 3, src: "Customization — Train on assistant messages only",
        expl: "Once a conversation is rendered through the template it is one token sequence; the markers are how the trainer knows which stretch of it is the assistant's response, so that the user and system messages can be left out of the loss. For known families such as Qwen3, TRL patches the markers in itself." },
      { q: "When SFTTrainer trains LoRA adapters rather than the whole model, the page suggests a higher learning rate, about 1e-4. Why?", opts: ["Because the frozen base weights need a stronger push to move", "Because only the new adapter parameters are being learned", "Because adapters are trained in lower precision than the model", "Because packing is turned on whenever PEFT is in use"], a: 1, src: "Customization — Train adapters with PEFT",
        expl: "With an adapter the pretrained weights stay frozen and only the small set of new parameters is trained from scratch, which tolerates — and needs — larger steps than nudging an already-trained network. The frozen weights do not move at any learning rate." },
      { q: "You instruction-tune a Qwen base model whose tokenizer already has a chat template, and the result never stops generating. What does the page say must be aligned?", opts: ["The learning rate, which must be lower for chat-tuned models", "The packing flag, which merges turns and hides their ends", "The EOS token, which must match the template's end-of-turn token", "The max_length, which must be None so that no turn is ever truncated"], a: 2, src: "Instruction tuning example",
        expl: "A base model with a predefined template does not need clone_chat_template, but its EOS token must be aligned with the template — eos_token in SFTConfig, for example the template's end-of-turn token — so that the model learns to emit the token that ends a reply." },
    ],
  },


  "ai310.1.2": {
    takeaway: "Quantization buys memory with precision: the weights are stored in fewer bits and accuracy is preserved as far as the method can manage — and which method fits is decided by your hardware and by what you need to do next (load, fine-tune, compile, save), which is what the compatibility table lays out.",
    beats: [
      { t: "What is being traded", d: "Weights are normally stored in full precision (fp32); half precision (fp16, bf16) is increasingly the norm for large models; some methods go on to integers such as int8 or int4. Fewer bits per weight means less memory to load and run the model, at some cost in accuracy that each method tries to minimise." },
      { t: "Calibrated or on the fly", d: "Some methods need a calibration step to reach greater accuracy and extreme compression (1-2 bits). Others quantize on the fly: point them at a full-precision checkpoint and they work out of the box." },
      { t: "Bits are a property of the method", d: "The bits column differs row by row: AQLM 1–2, AWQ 4, bitsandbytes 4 or 8, GPT-QModel 2, 3, 4 or 8, FBGEMM_FP8 only 8. And low bit widths are not reserved for calibrated methods — HIGGS, HQQ, optimum-quanto and Metal all quantize on the fly at 2 bits." },
      { t: "The table's columns are the tradeoffs", d: "For each method: on-the-fly or not; CPU, CUDA, ROCm, Metal and Intel GPU support; torch.compile; the bit widths; PEFT fine-tuning; whether Transformers can serialize the result. A method is a row of answers to those questions." },
      { t: "bitsandbytes: load and fine-tune in one step", d: "On the fly, 4 or 8 bits, CUDA and CPU and Intel GPU (partial on ROCm and Metal), PEFT fine-tuning supported, serializable. The fit when you want to take a downloaded model straight to a quantized fine-tune." },
      { t: "AWQ: 4-bit, prepared ahead", d: "Not on the fly — the model is quantized beforehand and loaded already quantized. 4 bits, CUDA, ROCm, CPU and Intel GPU, no Metal; PEFT and serialization supported." },
      { t: "GGUF: the llama.cpp format", d: "1 to 8 bits, runs on CPU, CUDA, Metal and Intel GPU, compiles — but it is not on the fly, does not support PEFT fine-tuning, and is not serializable with Transformers. It is for running a model, not for training it further." },
      { t: "Tools for doing it without code", d: "Community Spaces convert a repository for you (bitsandbytes, GGUF and MLX), and an AutoQuant notebook runs several methods; DeepLearning.AI's two short courses are the suggested start for newcomers." },
    ],
    worked: "Fix the constraints in order and let them eliminate rows. Hardware first (a Mac rules out AWQ; Metal favours GGUF), then the next step (fine-tuning with PEFT rules out GGUF; loading straight from a full-precision checkpoint needs an on-the-fly method such as bitsandbytes), then whether the result must be saved with Transformers. Whatever survives is the shortlist; only then compare bit widths.",
    watch: "Reading every coloured cell as a yes. Yellow is partial support — bitsandbytes on ROCm and Metal, torchao on Metal — and a '?' (AWQ, EETQ and Quark under torch.compile) means unknown, not supported. Check the exact cell for your hardware and your next step.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "According to the overview, what does quantization trade, and for what?", opts: ["Precision of the stored weights, for lower memory, while keeping as much accuracy as it can", "Context length, for a smaller model, while keeping the same weights at the same precision", "Training speed, for faster inference, by retraining the model in a lower-precision format", "Nothing at all: it is a lossless re-encoding of exactly the same weights into a smaller file"], a: 0, src: "Overview",
        expl: "Quantization stores the weights in a lower precision — fp32 to fp16 or bf16, or further to int8 or int4 — which lowers the memory needed to load and use the model. It is lossy: each method tries to preserve as much accuracy as possible, which is why some need calibration." },
      { q: "A model's weights take 16 GB stored in bf16. Ignoring the small overhead of scales and metadata, how many GB do the same weights take at int4?", num: 4, src: "Overview",
        expl: "bf16 is 16 bits per weight and int4 is 4, so each weight shrinks by a factor of 4: 16 GB / 4 = 4 GB. From fp32 (32 bits) the same model would have been 32 GB, which is why half precision is already the norm for large models." },
      { q: "According to the overview, what does a method's calibration step buy you?", opts: ["Support for PEFT fine-tuning, which on-the-fly methods lack", "The ability to run on a CPU rather than only on a GPU", "A result that Transformers can serialize and reload", "Greater accuracy, and extreme compression to 1–2 bits"], a: 3, src: "Overview",
        expl: "The overview: 'Some methods require calibration for greater accuracy and extreme compression (1-2 bits), while other methods work out of the box with on-the-fly quantization.' The table rules out the rest — bitsandbytes is on the fly and supports PEFT, and CPU support and serialization vary method by method. Nor is calibration the only way down to 2 bits: several on-the-fly methods offer it." },
      { q: "You want to load a freshly downloaded full-precision model in 4-bit on an NVIDIA GPU and fine-tune it with LoRA, with no separate quantization pass. Of these three, which does the table support?", opts: ["bitsandbytes, which quantizes on the fly and supports PEFT", "AWQ, since it is 4-bit and it supports PEFT fine-tuning too", "GGUF, since llama.cpp runs on CUDA as well as on a CPU", "None of them; all three need the model quantized first"], a: 0, src: "Overview, compatibility table",
        expl: "Two columns decide it. On the fly: bitsandbytes yes, AWQ and GGUF no — they load a model that was quantized beforehand. PEFT fine-tuning: bitsandbytes and AWQ yes, GGUF no. Only bitsandbytes passes both." },
      { q: "You want to run a quantized model locally on an Apple Silicon Mac. Of GGUF, AWQ and bitsandbytes, which does the table mark as fully supported on Metal?", opts: ["AWQ, because 4-bit kernels exist for every GPU backend", "GGUF, the llama.cpp format, which is supported on Metal", "bitsandbytes, because quantizing on the fly avoids kernels", "All three, because int4 arithmetic is the same on any chip"], a: 1, src: "Overview, compatibility table",
        expl: "Support is per backend, not per bit width. GGUF is green on Metal; AWQ is red there; bitsandbytes is only partial (yellow). Quantizing on the fly still needs kernels for the hardware it runs on." },
      { q: "You want 2-bit weights on an NVIDIA (CUDA) GPU and have no calibration data, so the method must quantize on the fly. Which of these does the table allow?", opts: ["AQLM, which goes down to 1 or 2 bits", "AWQ, the most common 4-bit format", "HIGGS, which supports 2 and 4 bits", "FBGEMM_FP8, which was built for CUDA"], a: 2, src: "Overview, compatibility table",
        expl: "Three columns decide it: on the fly, CUDA GPU, bits. AQLM reaches 2 bits but is not on the fly; AWQ is neither on the fly nor 2-bit; FBGEMM_FP8 is on the fly on CUDA but 8-bit only. HIGGS is green for both on the fly and CUDA, at 2 or 4 bits (HQQ and optimum-quanto would also qualify)." },
    ],
  },


  "ai310.1.3": {
    takeaway: "The harness turns 'how good is this model?' into a reproducible command — but only if you know what the number depends on: the backend and its parallel layout, whether the model exposes logprobs, how answers were extracted, and whose prompt procedure the task follows.",
    beats: [
      { t: "One framework, many benchmarks", d: "Over 60 standard academic benchmarks with hundreds of subtasks, run against models from transformers, vLLM, commercial APIs and more. It is the backend of Hugging Face's Open LLM Leaderboard, and it uses publicly available prompts so that results are reproducible and comparable between papers." },
      { t: "Install the core, then a backend", d: "The base package no longer pulls in transformers or torch; each backend is an extra — lm_eval[hf], lm_eval[vllm], lm_eval[api]. lm-eval ls tasks lists what can be run." },
      { t: "The hf backend passes model_args straight through", d: "Anything AutoModel accepts works: a local path, a Hub revision to evaluate a partially trained checkpoint, a dtype, a PEFT adapter (peft=PATH), delta weights, a GPTQ model. batch_size auto finds the largest batch that fits, and auto:N recomputes it N times for tasks whose examples vary a lot in length." },
      { t: "GGUF needs its tokenizer", d: "The hf backend can evaluate a GGUF file, but without a separate tokenizer it rebuilds one from the GGUF — which can take hours or hang. The gguf model type instead scores a llama.cpp server, computing loglikelihoods by exact teacher forcing; perplexity (loglikelihood_rolling) is not implemented there." },
      { t: "Two kinds of multi-GPU", d: "If the model fits on one GPU, accelerate launch gives every GPU its own full copy: K GPUs, K times faster. If it does not fit, parallelize=True splits the weights across GPUs. Both can be combined. Native tensor parallelism (tp_plan=auto) needs the number of key-value heads divisible by the TP degree. Multi-node is not supported natively." },
      { t: "vLLM and SGLang for speed, HF as the reference", d: "vLLM runs tensor parallel and data parallel together; each data-parallel replica is a ray actor holding tensor_parallel_size GPUs. Its outputs occasionally differ from Hugging Face's, which the harness treats as the reference — a comparator script checks one against the other." },
      { t: "No logprobs, no likelihood tasks", d: "A model that returns no logits or logprobs — most chat APIs — can run generate_until tasks only. loglikelihood, loglikelihood_rolling and multiple_choice need the probabilities of the prompt's continuations. For generative tasks on closed chat models, read a few outputs first (--limit 10) to confirm answer extraction and scoring work." },
      { t: "Keep the evidence", d: "--output_path saves results and --log_samples saves every response for post-hoc analysis; --use_cache resumes interrupted runs (per GPU count); --check_integrity verifies task data; --predict_only returns generations without scoring, for tasks that would execute untrusted code; write_out.py shows exactly what the model is fed." },
      { t: "Whose procedure a task follows", d: "In order: what people who train LLMs agree on, an official implementation, what evaluators agree on, then the harness's own pick among common ones. The point is to reduce the harm when people compare runs across papers — which the project discourages, and which happens anyway." },
    ],
    worked: "Treat an evaluation as an experiment with a config. Fix the backend, the model_args (revision, dtype, adapters), the parallel layout and the task version; run with --log_samples and --output_path; read some logged samples before reading the score; and when two backends disagree, check against the hf backend with the comparator before believing either.",
    watch: "Reading a generative-task score from a chat API without looking at its outputs. If the answer extraction does not match the model's response format, correct answers are scored as wrong, and the number measures the parser rather than the model — which is why the README asks for --limit 10 first.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "Evaluating a GGUF model through the hf backend seems to hang before the first task starts. What does the README identify as the cause and the fix?", opts: ["The GGUF file must be converted to safetensors first; convert it, then rerun", "The hf backend cannot read GGUF at all; serve the model with llama.cpp instead", "No separate tokenizer was passed, so one is being rebuilt from the GGUF file", "The batch size is too large for a quantized model; set batch_size to auto"], a: 2, src: "Basic Usage — Evaluating GGUF Models",
        expl: "Without a tokenizer path, Hugging Face reconstructs the tokenizer from the GGUF file, which can take hours or hang indefinitely. Passing tokenizer=/path/to/tokenizer in model_args cuts that from hours to seconds. The hf backend does read GGUF — via pretrained= the folder and gguf_file= the file." },
      { q: "Your model fits comfortably on one GPU and you have four. Which setup does the README use to make the evaluation about four times faster?", opts: ["parallelize=True, which splits the model's weights across all four GPUs", "tp_plan=auto, which shards the weights with native tensor parallelism", "batch_size auto:4, which recomputes the largest batch four times", "accelerate launch, so that each GPU loads its own full copy of the model"], a: 3, src: "Multi-GPU Evaluation with Hugging Face accelerate",
        expl: "Data parallelism: each GPU holds a separate full copy and takes a share of the examples, so K GPUs are K times faster. parallelize=True and tp_plan split ONE copy across GPUs — the tool for a model too large for one GPU, not a speed-up for one that fits." },
      { q: "With the vllm backend you pass tensor_parallel_size=2 and data_parallel_size=4. How many GPUs does the run reserve?", num: 8, src: "Tensor + Data Parallel and Optimized Inference with vLLM",
        expl: "Each of the 4 data-parallel replicas is dispatched as a separate ray actor, and each actor reserves tensor_parallel_size = 2 GPUs because its copy of the model is split across two. 4 x 2 = 8 GPUs: tensor parallelism splits a model, data parallelism multiplies it." },
      { q: "You want to score a chat-completions API model on a multiple-choice task that is scored by loglikelihood, and the API returns no logprobs. What does the README say?", opts: ["It runs, but the harness has to estimate the logprobs by sampling", "It cannot: without logprobs, only generate_until tasks can be run", "It runs once you pass the API model's own tokenizer to the harness", "It cannot, because API models only run perplexity-style tasks"], a: 1, src: "Model APIs and Inference Servers",
        expl: "Likelihood tasks rank answer options by the probability the model assigns to each continuation, so they need logits or logprobs. Models that supply none — the ChatCompletions and Anthropic rows say 'no logprobs' — can run generate_until tasks only." },
      { q: "Before trusting a generative-task score from a closed chat model, what does the README recommend doing?", opts: ["Run the whole task twice and average, as chat APIs sample at random", "Raise max_retries, because failed calls are scored as wrong answers", "Switch to the completions endpoint, the reference for every score", "Read a few outputs with --limit 10 to confirm answers are extracted"], a: 3, src: "Model APIs and Inference Servers (note on closed chat models)",
        expl: "A generative task is scored by extracting an answer from free text. If the extraction does not fit the model's response format, the score measures the parser, not the model — so look at about ten outputs first, and if needed use a system prompt in model_args to set the format." },
      { q: "When implementing a task, the harness prefers the procedure LLM trainers agree on, then an official implementation, before its own choice. What is that order for?", opts: ["So its numbers agree with other groups' runs, as people compare across papers anyway", "So every task scores a little higher, which makes the leaderboard easier to read", "So that no prompt is ever made public, which keeps tasks out of training data", "So the harness can skip its own validation, since agreed procedures are known correct"], a: 0, src: "Contributing — Implementing new tasks",
        expl: "The stated aim is to decrease the harm when people inevitably compare runs across different papers, a practice the project discourages. Its prompts are deliberately public — that is what makes results reproducible — and the order is a guideline that can be overruled." },
    ],
  },


  "ai310.1.4": {
    takeaway: "vLLM's speed comes from treating the KV cache as managed memory — paged into fixed-size blocks, shared between requests by hashing whole prefixes, evicted least-recently-used — and its usefulness from serving all of that behind an OpenAI-compatible API.",
    beats: [
      { t: "What vLLM is", d: "A library for LLM inference and serving, begun at Berkeley's Sky Computing Lab and now a large open-source project. Three ways in: run models (the Quickstart), build applications (the User Guide), or build vLLM itself (the Developer Guide)." },
      { t: "Why it is fast", d: "State-of-the-art serving throughput; efficient management of attention key and value memory with PagedAttention; continuous batching, chunked prefill and prefix caching; CUDA graphs; quantization from FP8 to INT4, GPTQ, AWQ and GGUF; optimized attention kernels; speculative decoding; and prefill, decode and encode that can be disaggregated." },
      { t: "Why it is flexible", d: "Hugging Face models (200+ architectures, from decoder-only to MoE, state-space and multimodal), parallel sampling and beam search, tensor, pipeline, data, expert and context parallelism, streaming, structured outputs, tool calling, multi-LoRA, an OpenAI-compatible server, and a wide range of hardware." },
      { t: "Offline batched inference", d: "LLM loads the model and engine; SamplingParams sets temperature, top-p and the rest; llm.generate adds the prompts to the engine's waiting queue and runs it for throughput. Unless told otherwise, vLLM applies the model repository's generation_config.json; generation_config='vllm' restores vLLM's own defaults." },
      { t: "generate is not chat", d: "llm.generate does not apply the model's chat template. For an Instruct or Chat model, apply the template yourself with the tokenizer, or call llm.chat with OpenAI-style message lists." },
      { t: "Online serving", d: "vllm serve MODEL starts a server on localhost:8000 implementing the OpenAI API — list models, completions, chat completions — so existing OpenAI clients work by changing the base URL. One model at a time; --api-key makes it check keys (several, for rotation). The attention backend is chosen automatically, or set with --attention-backend." },
      { t: "Prefix caching: reuse, do not recompute", d: "The KV-cache blocks of processed requests are kept and reused when a new request starts with the same prefix — almost a free lunch, since outputs do not change. Each block's hash combines its parent block's hash, its own tokens, and extras such as LoRA IDs, image hashes or a cache salt; only full blocks are cached." },
      { t: "A block pool with an LRU free queue", d: "All blocks are allocated up front as a pool, each with a reference count. A request reusing cached blocks 'touches' them out of the free queue; new blocks are popped from its head, evicting whatever cached block was least recently used. A finished request's blocks are freed in reverse order, so its last block — hashing the longest prefix, least likely to be reused — goes first." },
      { t: "Isolation for shared deployments", d: "A per-request cache_salt is folded into the first block's hash, so only requests with the same salt can share cached blocks. That blocks timing attacks in which one tenant infers another's cached content from latency." },
    ],
    worked: "To serve a model: vllm serve it, then point the openai client's base_url at http://localhost:8000/v1 and use completions or chat completions unchanged. To get prefix-cache hits, put the shared part of every request — system prompt, long document — at the very start, identical token for token, because reuse is decided block by block from the first token.",
    watch: "Expecting prefix caching to reuse any shared text. A block is reusable only if it is full and every token before it matches too, so a shared passage after a differing first line, or a partial final block, is recomputed.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "The landing page lists PagedAttention first among what makes vLLM fast. What does it manage efficiently?", opts: ["The model weights, paging each layer in from disk as it is needed", "The memory that holds the attention keys and values for requests", "The queue of incoming requests, batching them as they arrive", "The precision of the attention scores, storing them as FP8"], a: 1, src: "Welcome to vLLM — vLLM is fast with",
        expl: "PagedAttention is 'efficient management of attention key and value memory' — the KV cache, held in blocks rather than one contiguous slab per request. Batching requests as they arrive is a separate feature (continuous batching), and so is quantization." },
      { q: "You load an Instruct model with LLM and call llm.generate on plain prompt strings, and the answers look wrong. What does the quickstart point out?", opts: ["generate needs SamplingParams, or else it decodes greedily from the prompt", "Instruct models can be used only through the OpenAI-compatible server", "generate does not apply the chat template: apply it, or use llm.chat", "The prompts must be tokenized first, since generate expects token IDs"], a: 2, src: "Quickstart — Offline Batched Inference",
        expl: "An Instruct model was trained on text in its chat template, and llm.generate passes your string through as-is. Apply the template with the tokenizer (apply_chat_template with add_generation_prompt) or call llm.chat with message lists. The server, by contrast, uses the tokenizer's template by default." },
      { q: "You create LLM without passing SamplingParams, and the sampling defaults turn out not to be vLLM's own. Why?", opts: ["vLLM applies the model repository's generation_config.json by default", "vLLM reuses the defaults of the last server started on that machine", "vLLM picks sampling defaults at random unless a seed is supplied", "vLLM reads its defaults from the OpenAI API that it is imitating"], a: 0, src: "Quickstart — Offline Batched Inference; Online Serving",
        expl: "If the Hugging Face repository has a generation_config.json, vLLM uses the sampling values the model's creator recommends. To use vLLM's defaults instead, pass generation_config='vllm' to LLM, or --generation-config vllm to vllm serve." },
      { q: "Two requests contain an identical 4-token block, but the tokens before that block differ. Can the second request reuse the first one's cached copy of the block?", opts: ["Yes, because a block's hash is computed from its own four tokens only", "Yes, but only if both requests use the same sampling parameters", "No, because cached blocks are never shared between two requests", "No, because each block's hash folds in its parent's, so its prefix"], a: 3, src: "Automatic Prefix Caching",
        expl: "vLLM hashes each block by its own tokens AND the hash of the block before it, so a block is identified by everything up to its end. That is exactly right for a KV cache: a token's keys and values depend on all the tokens before it, so the same four tokens after a different prefix have different K and V." },
      { q: "Block size 16. An earlier request with a 64-token prompt is fully cached. A new 50-token prompt matches it in its first 40 tokens and then differs. How many of the new prompt's tokens are served from the prefix cache?", num: 32, src: "Automatic Prefix Caching — Example",
        expl: "Only full blocks that match token for token, from the start, are hits. Tokens 1-16 and 17-32 match; the third block (33-48) matches only 8 of its 16 tokens, so it misses — and every block after a miss has a different parent hash. 2 x 16 = 32 tokens hit; 18 are computed." },
      { q: "When a request finishes, vLLM adds its freed blocks to the free queue in reverse order, so its last block is evicted first. Why that order?", opts: ["The last block was the one written most recently, so it is the coldest", "Its last block hashes the longest prefix, so it is least likely reused", "The last block is usually partly full, so it can never be cached", "Reversing the order makes queue moves O(1) instead of O(n)"], a: 1, src: "Automatic Prefix Caching — Free",
        expl: "A later block is identified by a longer prefix, so fewer future requests will match it; the first blocks — a shared system prompt, say — are the likeliest hits. Evicting from the head of the free queue then throws away the least useful blocks first. The O(1) moves come from the doubly linked list, not from the order." },
    ],
  },


  "ai310.1.5": {
    takeaway: "An LLM can only emit text, so 'tool use' is a protocol: the system prompt describes the tools, the model writes an action in an agreed format and stops, the agent parses and runs it, and the result goes back into the prompt as an observation — Thought, Action, Observation, in a loop until the task is done.",
    beats: [
      { t: "An agent is a model with a body", d: "A system that uses an AI model to interact with its environment to reach a user's objective. The brain — usually an LLM — reasons, plans and decides which action to take; the body is the set of tools it has been given, and it can do nothing those tools do not allow." },
      { t: "Agency is a spectrum", d: "Simple processor (output changes nothing), router (output picks a branch), tool caller (output picks the function and its arguments), multi-step agent (output decides whether the loop continues), multi-agent (one agentic workflow starts another)." },
      { t: "The brain predicts tokens until EOS", d: "LLMs are mostly decoders predicting the next token from the previous ones, autoregressively, until they emit the end-of-sequence token. Each model has its own special tokens to open and close messages and turns." },
      { t: "Messages are a UI abstraction", d: "Before the model sees a conversation, every message is concatenated into one prompt by the model's chat template; the model does not remember, it rereads everything each turn. The system message sets behaviour and, for an agent, lists the tools, the action format and how to lay out thoughts." },
      { t: "A tool is a function the LLM is told about", d: "It needs a description of what it does, a callable, typed arguments and, optionally, typed outputs. Good tools complement the model — a calculator for arithmetic, a search for anything after its training data. A @tool decorator can build the description from the function's name, docstring and type hints; MCP standardises the interface across frameworks." },
      { t: "Thought, Action, Observation", d: "The LLM decides the next step, the agent calls a tool with arguments, and the result is appended to the prompt for the model to reflect on — a while loop that runs until the objective is met. Chain-of-Thought reasons step by step without tools; ReAct interleaves reasoning with actions and observations; models such as DeepSeek-R1 are trained to think in delimited reasoning tokens." },
      { t: "Actions: JSON, code, or function calls", d: "A JSON agent writes the action as a JSON object; a code agent writes an executable code block, more expressive (loops, conditionals, libraries) but a security risk to run; a function-calling agent is a JSON agent fine-tuned to emit a new message per action. All rely on stop and parse: the model stops once the action is complete, and a parser extracts the tool and its arguments." },
      { t: "The failure the dummy agent shows", d: "Left to generate freely, the model writes its Action and then an Observation of its own — invented weather, with no tool run. The fix is to stop generation at 'Observation:', run the real function, append its output as the Observation, and resume." },
      { t: "Then a real framework", d: "smolagents' CodeAgent does the same loop with code actions: tools defined with type hints and docstrings that describe every argument, a final_answer tool, and a cap on steps (max_steps)." },
    ],
    worked: "Build the loop by hand once. Write a system prompt that lists one tool and the exact action format, call the model with stop set to the observation marker, parse the JSON it produced, run the function, append 'Observation:' and the real result to the assistant message, and call the model again. Every agent framework is this, plus error handling.",
    watch: "Trusting text after the action. A model that is not stopped will happily write the observation itself — fluent, plausible and fabricated — and then a final answer built on it. Stop at the observation marker, and treat only what the tool returned as the source of truth.",
    concepts: [],
    checks: [
      { q: "An LLM's output chooses which function to run and with what arguments, but your program runs it once and stops. Where does that sit on the unit's spectrum of agency?", opts: ["Router: the output chooses between two fixed paths in the program", "Simple processor: the output has no effect on what the program does", "Tool caller: the output decides which function runs, and with what", "Multi-step agent: the output controls whether the loop carries on"], a: 2, src: "What is an Agent? — The spectrum of agency",
        expl: "Choosing the function and its arguments is the tool-caller level (run_function(llm_chosen_tool, llm_chosen_args)). It becomes a multi-step agent only when the model's output also decides whether to keep iterating — the while loop of Thought, Action, Observation." },
      { q: "Three turns into a chat with an agent, how does the LLM 'remember' what the user said in the first turn?", opts: ["Every message is concatenated into one prompt, which it rereads in full", "It stores earlier turns in its weights, updating them after every reply", "The system message keeps a summary that the model rewrites every turn", "It does not: each turn is answered from the newest user message only"], a: 0, src: "Messages and Special Tokens",
        expl: "Chat is a UI abstraction. The chat template concatenates all messages, with the model's special tokens marking each turn, into a single prompt; the model reads it in full every time. That is also how tool results get in: they are appended as messages and the whole conversation is sent again." },
      { q: "When an agent 'uses' a weather tool, what does the LLM itself actually do?", opts: ["It calls the weather API itself, over a connection the framework opens", "It writes text describing the call; the agent parses it and runs the tool", "It recalls the weather from its training data, formatted as a tool result", "It hands the conversation to the tool, which then writes the final reply"], a: 1, src: "What are Tools? — How do tools work?",
        expl: "LLMs take text in and give text out; they cannot call anything. Providing a tool means describing it in the system prompt so the model can write a text invocation such as call weather_tool('Paris'). The agent executes it, appends the result, and sends the updated conversation back to the model." },
      { q: "A question needs today's exchange rate. Why does the unit's comparison point to ReAct rather than Chain-of-Thought for it?", opts: ["ReAct reasons in more steps, and more steps make the arithmetic reliable", "Chain-of-Thought is a training technique, so it needs a fine-tuned model", "ReAct interleaves actions and observations; CoT reasons without tools", "Chain-of-Thought cannot be used with chat templates or system messages"], a: 2, src: "Thought: Internal Reasoning and the ReAct Approach",
        expl: "Both reason step by step, but Chain-of-Thought stays inside the model — good for logic and maths — while ReAct alternates thoughts with tool actions and their observations, which is what info-seeking tasks need. Both are prompting strategies; training a model to think in delimited reasoning tokens is the training-level technique." },
      { q: "A code agent writes a Python block as its action instead of a JSON object. What does the unit give as the gain and the cost?", opts: ["Gain: loops, conditionals and libraries; cost: running generated code is risky", "Gain: no parser is needed at all; cost: the model must be fine-tuned for code", "Gain: actions run faster than JSON; cost: it can no longer call outside APIs", "Gain: no system prompt is needed; cost: stop-and-parse no longer applies"], a: 0, src: "Actions — Code Agents",
        expl: "Code can express loops, conditionals, nested functions and library calls that a JSON blob cannot, and is easier to debug. But executing LLM-generated code invites everything from prompt injection to harmful code, so the unit recommends a framework with safeguards. Code agents still stop and parse: the block is delimited and its end is signalled." },
      { q: "In the dummy agent, the model writes an Action and then, by itself, an 'Observation' with plausible weather — though no tool ran. What fix does the unit apply?", opts: ["Lower the temperature, so the model stops inventing its observations", "Put the weather in the system prompt, so no tool call is ever needed", "Ask the model to mark invented text, and filter that out afterwards", "Stop at 'Observation:', then run the tool and insert its real output"], a: 3, src: "Dummy Agent Library",
        expl: "The model was hallucinating — generating an observation rather than receiving one. Passing stop=['Observation:'] ends generation right after the action; the agent then runs get_weather, appends 'Observation:' and the real result to the assistant message, and resumes generation from there." },
    ],
  },

});
